// Extra generated flashcards per chapter (build_cards.py).
window.EXTRA_CARDS = {"1": [{"q": "How did most Native American societies view land before European contact?", "a": "As a shared communal resource rather than private property, valuing community obligation and generosity over individual accumulation."}, {"q": "What was Tenochtitlan?", "a": "The island capital of the Aztec empire, one of the world's largest cities around 1500 with about 250,000 people; the Spanish rebuilt it as Mexico City."}, {"q": "What made Cahokia significant?", "a": "A Mississippi valley city near modern St. Louis holding 10,000-30,000 people around 1200, the largest settlement in what is now the U.S. before the 1800s."}, {"q": "Which five nations formed the Iroquois Great League of Peace?", "a": "The Mohawk, Oneida, Cayuga, Seneca, and Onondaga, whose alliance brought stability and unity in dealings with Europeans."}, {"q": "What is a matrilineal society, and why did Europeans misread it?", "a": "A kinship system where children belonged to the mother's line and women owned homes and did most farming; Europeans saw it as disorder."}, {"q": "Why did European ideas of freedom differ from Native ones?", "a": "European freedom rested on hierarchy, obedience, coverture in marriage, and an established church, unlike Native emphasis on communal obligation."}, {"q": "How did Europeans use cultural differences to justify conquest?", "a": "They judged Native practices around land, gender, and religion as evidence of savagery, using this to rationalize seizing land and ruling peoples."}, {"q": "What primarily drove European overseas expansion?", "a": "The search for a sea route to Asian trade to bypass Muslim and Italian middlemen, combined with religious zeal and national rivalry."}, {"q": "What was the reconquista, and why is 1492 doubly important to Spain?", "a": "Spain's centuries-long campaign to retake Iberia from the Moors, completed in 1492, the same year Ferdinand and Isabella sponsored Columbus."}, {"q": "How did the caravel enable exploration?", "a": "This Portuguese-developed ship, with tools like the compass and quadrant, made long voyages down Africa's coast and across the Atlantic feasible."}, {"q": "Who was Christopher Columbus and what did his voyages begin?", "a": "A Genoese mariner sailing for Spain who reached the Caribbean in 1492 seeking Asia, beginning permanent contact between the hemispheres."}, {"q": "What was the Columbian Exchange?", "a": "The transfer of plants, animals, people, and microbes between the Old and New Worlds after 1492, reshaping diets but bringing devastating epidemics."}, {"q": "Why did European diseases matter so much to the conquest?", "a": "They caused the worst demographic collapse in history, killing most Native people and weakening resistance so Europeans could seize control."}, {"q": "Besides disease, what helped Cortes and Pizarro topple the Aztec and Inca?", "a": "Steel weapons, gunpowder, and alliances with Indian peoples resentful of Aztec and Inca rule."}, {"q": "What role did Indian labor play in Spain's empire?", "a": "Indians supplied most labor under systems like the encomienda and repartimiento in Spain's vast, urban, Catholic empire."}, {"q": "What was the repartimiento system, and how did it differ from the encomienda?", "a": "A 1550 arrangement in which Indians stayed legally free and earned wages but still owed a yearly quota of labor, replacing the harsher encomienda."}, {"q": "Who was Bartolome de Las Casas?", "a": "A Dominican priest who exposed Spanish cruelty toward Indians; his campaign helped produce the 1542 New Laws ending Indian enslavement."}, {"q": "What was the Black Legend and who exploited it?", "a": "The image of Spain as uniquely cruel, spread partly by Las Casas; rivals like England used it to cast their own empires as bearers of freedom."}, {"q": "Who were the mestizos?", "a": "People of mixed Spanish and Indian ancestry who became a major part of Spanish America's population, reflecting its blended culture."}, {"q": "What happened in the Pueblo Revolt of 1680?", "a": "Pueblo Indians led by Pope drove Spanish colonists out of New Mexico for over a decade, the most successful Indian revolt in North American history."}, {"q": "How did the French empire in North America differ from Spain's?", "a": "New France was thinly settled and commercial, based on the fur trade and Indian alliances, giving natives comparatively more autonomy."}, {"q": "What was distinctive about New Netherland?", "a": "A Dutch commercial colony on the Hudson notable for its diverse population, relative religious tolerance, and legal rights for women, though not democratic."}, {"q": "Why did the fur trade give Indians more autonomy under France and the Netherlands?", "a": "Those colonies depended on Native partners to supply furs, making cooperation rather than displacement essential to their profit."}, {"q": "When did agriculture emerge in the Americas, and why did it matter?", "a": "Around 7000 BC in Mexico and the Andes, making settled Indian civilizations possible."}, {"q": "What did Bartolomeu Dias accomplish in 1487?", "a": "He rounded the Cape of Good Hope at Africa's southern tip, a key step in Portugal's quest for a sea route to Asia."}, {"q": "How did the Protestant Reformation affect Spanish colonization?", "a": "Luther's 1517 Ninety-Five Theses launched the Reformation, intensifying Catholic Spain's missionary drive in the Americas."}, {"q": "Who founded Quebec and when?", "a": "Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec in 1608, establishing the base of the French fur-trading empire."}, {"q": "What was Santa Fe's significance?", "a": "Established by Spain in 1610 as the capital of New Mexico, it was the first permanent European settlement in the Southwest."}, {"q": "Why is there no single 'Indian' identity before contact?", "a": "Native societies were remarkably diverse in language, politics, religion, and economy, ranging from empires to small farming villages."}, {"q": "How did colonization intertwine freedom and slavery from the start?", "a": "The expansion of freedom and opportunity for some Europeans depended on the enslavement, disease, and dispossession of Indians and Africans."}], "2": [{"q": "Why did England enter the colonial race late?", "a": "Religious turmoil after Henry VIII broke with Rome and England's brutal conquest of Ireland delayed its overseas ventures until the early 1600s."}, {"q": "How did the conquest of Ireland shape English colonization?", "a": "It previewed tactics of viewing natives as savage and separating settlers from them, later applied to American Indians."}, {"q": "What was the enclosure movement, and how did it fuel emigration?", "a": "Landlords fenced in common farmland, evicting thousands of peasants and creating a displaced poor population willing to migrate to America."}, {"q": "What happened to the Roanoke colony?", "a": "Walter Raleigh's 1580s settlement off North Carolina vanished without explanation, showing colonization needed resources beyond a single sponsor."}, {"q": "What was the Virginia Company?", "a": "A private joint-stock corporation chartered in 1606 that financed and governed early Virginia for profit; it lost its charter in 1624."}, {"q": "Why did early Jamestown nearly collapse?", "a": "Disease, starvation, and poor leadership devastated the colony until tobacco provided a profitable staple crop."}, {"q": "How did John Smith help Jamestown survive?", "a": "His strict 'no work, no food' discipline held the colony together, and his writings promoted America as a place ordinary men could own land."}, {"q": "How did tobacco transform Virginia?", "a": "Introduced by John Rolfe, it enriched planters and the crown, drove demand for servant labor, and fueled a scramble for Indian land."}, {"q": "What was the headright system?", "a": "Virginia's 1618 policy granting 50 acres to anyone paying a person's passage, rewarding servant importers with large estates."}, {"q": "What was the House of Burgesses?", "a": "Created in Virginia in 1619, it was the first elected legislature in English America, though voting was limited to landowners."}, {"q": "What caused the Uprising of 1622 and what were its effects?", "a": "Opechancanough led a surprise attack killing about a quarter of Virginia's settlers; its failure ruined the Virginia Company and hardened anti-Indian policy."}, {"q": "Who were indentured servants and how did they differ from slaves?", "a": "Migrants who traded years of bound labor for passage; they were about two-thirds of settlers and could eventually gain freedom dues, unlike slaves."}, {"q": "How did Chesapeake society develop demographically?", "a": "Men greatly outnumbered women, death rates stayed high, and a planter gentry engrossed most land and power in a tobacco economy."}, {"q": "Why was Maryland founded, and by whom?", "a": "Chartered in 1632 as a proprietary refuge for Catholics under the Calvert family, it followed a tobacco path amid religious conflict."}, {"q": "Who were the Puritans and what did they seek?", "a": "English Protestants influenced by Calvin who thought the Church of England kept too much Catholic ritual; they sought to build a purified society."}, {"q": "How did the Great Migration shape New England?", "a": "About 21,000 Puritans, largely in families, moved to Massachusetts between 1629-1642, creating a stable, rapidly growing population."}, {"q": "Why did New England grow more stably than the Chesapeake?", "a": "Puritans migrated in family groups with balanced sex ratios into a healthy climate, unlike the male-heavy, disease-ridden tobacco colonies."}, {"q": "What did Puritans mean by 'moral liberty'?", "a": "Freedom to do only what was godly and right, exercised under authority, as distinguished from sinful 'natural' liberty to do as one pleased."}, {"q": "Who was John Winthrop?", "a": "The long-serving governor of Massachusetts Bay who defined the Puritan distinction between natural liberty and godly moral liberty."}, {"q": "What was the Mayflower Compact?", "a": "A 1620 agreement by the Pilgrims' adult men to obey laws made by their own chosen representatives, the first written framework of government in English America."}, {"q": "Why was Roger Williams banished, and what did he found?", "a": "Banished in 1636 for urging separation of church and state, he founded Rhode Island, which had no established church and welcomed dissenters."}, {"q": "Why was Anne Hutchinson tried and expelled?", "a": "She charged that Massachusetts ministers preached salvation by works and claimed direct revelation from God, leading to her 1637 banishment."}, {"q": "Did Puritan 'moral liberty' include religious toleration? Explain.", "a": "No; it meant freedom to do what was godly under authority, so dissenters like Williams and Hutchinson were banished rather than tolerated."}, {"q": "What was the outcome of the Pequot War of 1637?", "a": "Colonial forces destroyed the Pequots, crushing native resistance in New England and asserting settler supremacy."}, {"q": "What was the Maryland Toleration Act?", "a": "A 1649 law guaranteeing free worship to all Christians in Maryland, a landmark born of Catholic-Protestant conflict."}, {"q": "How did the English Civil War influence the colonies?", "a": "It spread new debates over the rights of freeborn Englishmen, religious toleration, and even democracy that echoed across English America."}, {"q": "How was access to land linked to English ideas of liberty?", "a": "Owning land was seen as the foundation of liberty, but settlers secured it by dispossessing Indians rather than incorporating them."}, {"q": "What year did Jamestown become a royal colony and why?", "a": "In 1624, after the Virginia Company lost its charter following the disastrous 1622 uprising."}, {"q": "Compare labor systems in the Chesapeake and New England.", "a": "The Chesapeake relied on indentured servants for tobacco, while New England depended on family farms, fishing, and timber."}, {"q": "What two notable events both occurred in Virginia in 1619?", "a": "The House of Burgesses first met and the first Africans arrived in the colony."}], "3": [{"q": "What is mercantilism?", "a": "An economic doctrine that governments should regulate trade so exports exceed imports and colonies enrich the mother country with raw materials and markets."}, {"q": "What did the Navigation Acts require?", "a": "Beginning in 1651, that key 'enumerated' colonial goods like tobacco and sugar travel in English ships through English ports, aimed at Dutch trade."}, {"q": "How did lax enforcement of trade laws affect colonists?", "a": "Loose enforcement let colonists prosper and trade widely within the mercantilist system, a practice later called salutary neglect."}, {"q": "How did England acquire New York?", "a": "It seized New Netherland from the Dutch in 1664 and renamed the colony New York."}, {"q": "What kind of economy did Carolina develop?", "a": "Founded as a Barbados offshoot, it built a rice-and-slave economy in its Lower South region."}, {"q": "Who founded Pennsylvania and what was its vision?", "a": "William Penn founded it in 1681 as a Quaker 'holy experiment' offering religious liberty, cheap land, and initially fair dealings with Indians."}, {"q": "What did the Society of Friends (Quakers) believe?", "a": "That God's 'inner light' dwells in every person; they embraced spiritual equality, pacifism, and eventually opposition to slavery."}, {"q": "What was the Covenant Chain?", "a": "An alliance formed in the 1670s between New York's governor and the Iroquois in which English and Iroquois ambitions reinforced each other."}, {"q": "What was King Philip's War and its outcome?", "a": "A 1675-1676 uprising led by the Wampanoag Metacom; its defeat devastated New England but permanently broke the region's Indian power."}, {"q": "What caused Bacon's Rebellion?", "a": "In 1676 Nathaniel Bacon rallied small farmers, servants, and landless men against Governor Berkeley's Indian policy and corrupt rule."}, {"q": "How did Bacon's Rebellion push Virginia toward slavery?", "a": "The revolt of discontented landless whites frightened elites, who turned to enslaved Africans as a more controllable labor force."}, {"q": "How was slavery constructed gradually through law?", "a": "Statutes made status hereditary through the mother, ended conversion as a path to freedom, and culminated in the 1705 Virginia slave code."}, {"q": "What did the Virginia slave code of 1705 do?", "a": "It defined enslaved people as property fully subject to owners and separated black and white courts, completing Virginia's shift to a slave society."}, {"q": "What was the Glorious Revolution?", "a": "The 1688 overthrow of Catholic James II for William and Mary, confirming parliamentary supremacy and liberty as an Englishman's birthright."}, {"q": "What did the English Bill of Rights establish?", "a": "The 1689 law listed Parliament's powers, like control of taxation, and protections like trial by jury, defining English liberty."}, {"q": "What was the Dominion of New England?", "a": "James II's 1686-1689 merger of New England, New York, and the Jerseys under Edmund Andros without an elected assembly; colonists dismantled it in 1689."}, {"q": "How did the Glorious Revolution echo in the colonies?", "a": "It triggered the collapse of the Dominion, Leisler's Rebellion in New York, and a Protestant uprising in Maryland."}, {"q": "What was Leisler's Rebellion?", "a": "A 1689 seizure of power in New York by militia captain Jacob Leisler after the Glorious Revolution; his execution polarized the colony for a generation."}, {"q": "What happened in the Salem witch trials?", "a": "A 1692 wave of witchcraft accusations led to nineteen hangings before the governor halted them, discrediting witch prosecutions in America."}, {"q": "How much did the mainland population grow between 1700 and 1770?", "a": "It increased nearly tenfold, swelled by German, Scotch-Irish, convict, and enslaved African migrants."}, {"q": "Who were redemptioners?", "a": "Indentured families, many German, who received Atlantic passage in exchange for a promise to work off the cost after arriving."}, {"q": "What was the Walking Purchase of 1737?", "a": "A Pennsylvania land fraud in which hired runners marked off a huge cession from the Lenni Lenape, ending the colony's peaceful Indian relations."}, {"q": "What was the backcountry?", "a": "A fast-growing inland region from central Pennsylvania down the Shenandoah Valley into the Carolinas, settled by Scotch-Irish and German families."}, {"q": "How did the eighteenth-century colonies become so diverse?", "a": "Massive immigration from Germany, Ireland, Scotland, and Africa created an ethnically and religiously varied population unlike England's."}, {"q": "What is Anglicization?", "a": "The process by which a colonial elite of merchants and planters modeled itself on the English gentry in manners, goods, and culture."}, {"q": "Describe the social structure of eighteenth-century free colonial society.", "a": "An Anglicized elite topped a broad middle rank of landowning farmers, with a growing poor below and enslaved people beneath all."}, {"q": "How did the Restoration affect England's American empire?", "a": "After 1660 England expanded the empire under mercantilist principles, passing Navigation Acts and founding several new colonies."}, {"q": "How did the Middle Colonies' economy differ from other regions?", "a": "They were grain-exporting societies, distinct from New England towns and the plantation South."}, {"q": "Why did expanding settlement steadily erode Indian land and autonomy?", "a": "Rapid population growth and land hunger, sometimes via fraud like the Walking Purchase, pushed settlement into Indian territory."}, {"q": "How did race become the central social divide in the Chesapeake?", "a": "Law converted slavery into a hereditary, racial institution, hardening the line between free whites and enslaved blacks."}], "4": [{"q": "What was the central paradox of the eighteenth-century British Atlantic?", "a": "An expanding rhetoric of British liberty coincided with the peak of the Atlantic slave trade dominated by British merchants."}, {"q": "What was the triangular trade?", "a": "Atlantic routes carrying manufactured goods, enslaved captives, and plantation staples in a circuit linking Europe, Africa, and the Americas."}, {"q": "What was the Middle Passage?", "a": "The brutal Atlantic crossing of enslaved Africans, the middle leg of the triangular trade, in which roughly one in five people died in transit."}, {"q": "Who was Olaudah Equiano?", "a": "An enslaved African who bought his freedom and published a widely read 1789 autobiography attacking slavery and the notion of African inferiority."}, {"q": "What were the three regional slave systems in mainland North America?", "a": "Chesapeake tobacco plantations, South Carolina and Georgia rice and indigo plantations, and small-scale nonplantation slavery in the North."}, {"q": "What was the task system?", "a": "A labor arrangement on Lower South rice plantations giving each slave a daily quota, after which their time was their own, unlike Chesapeake gang labor."}, {"q": "Compare the task system with Chesapeake gang labor.", "a": "The task system let slaves control time after finishing a daily quota, while gang labor kept Chesapeake slaves under constant supervision."}, {"q": "What was the Stono Rebellion?", "a": "A 1739 uprising in which South Carolina slaves, many Kongo-born, seized weapons and marched toward Spanish Florida; its defeat brought a harsher slave code."}, {"q": "How did enslaved people create African-American culture?", "a": "Out of many African peoples they blended African, European, and American elements into new shared cultures despite the brutality of bondage."}, {"q": "How did enslaved people resist bondage?", "a": "By running away, working slowly, preserving their culture, and occasionally in armed risings like the 1739 Stono Rebellion."}, {"q": "What is republicanism?", "a": "A political outlook holding that liberty depended on active civic participation by economically independent, virtuous citizens serving the public good."}, {"q": "What is liberalism in this era?", "a": "Lockean philosophy locating freedom in individual natural rights and a social contract that limits government's power over life, property, and conscience."}, {"q": "Contrast republicanism and liberalism.", "a": "Republicanism stressed public virtue and civic participation, while liberalism stressed individual natural rights and limits on government."}, {"q": "What did John Locke argue?", "a": "In Two Treatises of Government he held that legitimate authority rests on consent and that people may resist rulers who violate their natural rights."}, {"q": "What was salutary neglect and its effect?", "a": "Britain's informal loose enforcement of colonial regulations, which let American assemblies claim ever-greater control over local affairs."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the Zenger trial?", "a": "The 1735 jury acquittal of printer John Peter Zenger for attacking the governor strengthened the idea that publishing the truth should be protected."}, {"q": "What was the Enlightenment?", "a": "A transatlantic movement applying reason and science to society and religion; American Deists like Franklin saw God's design in natural law."}, {"q": "What was the Great Awakening?", "a": "Emotional revivals of the 1730s-1740s led by Jonathan Edwards and George Whitefield that split churches and encouraged independent judgment."}, {"q": "How did the Great Awakening encourage colonists to question authority?", "a": "By emphasizing personal conversion and letting ordinary people judge ministers for themselves, it democratized religion and undermined deference to elites."}, {"q": "Who was Junipero Serra?", "a": "A Franciscan friar who founded California's first mission at San Diego in 1769; his missions converted many Indians but relied on forced labor."}, {"q": "Why were Spain's and France's North American colonies weaker than Britain's?", "a": "Spain's borderlands from Florida to California stayed thinly settled, and New France, though vast, was badly underpopulated."}, {"q": "What sparked the Seven Years' War?", "a": "Anglo-French rivalry over the Ohio Valley ignited fighting in 1754, expanding into a global conflict also called the French and Indian War."}, {"q": "What did the 1763 Peace of Paris accomplish?", "a": "Britain's victory expelled France from North America, redrawing the imperial map and transforming the global balance of power."}, {"q": "What was the Albany Plan of Union?", "a": "Franklin's 1754 proposal for a Grand Council of colonial delegates to manage defense and Indian affairs; assemblies rejected it to protect their power."}, {"q": "Why did Britain's victory hurt Native Americans?", "a": "The French withdrawal ended balance-of-power diplomacy, so Indians could no longer play rival empires against each other for leverage."}, {"q": "What was Pontiac's Rebellion?", "a": "A 1763 pan-Indian assault on British forts in the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes, inspired partly by the Delaware prophet Neolin."}, {"q": "What did the Proclamation of 1763 do, and why did colonists resent it?", "a": "It barred settlement west of the Appalachians to calm the frontier, angering land-hungry settlers and speculators."}, {"q": "How did the Seven Years' War set the stage for the imperial crisis?", "a": "Wartime debts pushed Britain to tax the colonies, while frontier tensions and the Proclamation Line fueled colonial resentment."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the 1712 and 1741 events in New York City?", "a": "A 1712 slave uprising and a 1741 conspiracy panic showed slavery's presence and tensions even in the northern colonies."}, {"q": "Why did Georgia's founding under Oglethorpe stand out?", "a": "Chartered in 1732, Georgia initially banned slavery, though the prohibition was later lifted as the plantation model spread."}], "5": [{"q": "What war left Britain deeply in debt and prompted new colonial taxes?", "a": "The Seven Years' War (French and Indian War). Its costs led Parliament to abandon loose oversight and raise revenue directly from the colonies."}, {"q": "What did the Sugar Act of 1764 actually do?", "a": "It lowered the molasses duty but cracked down on smuggling and shifted trials to juryless admiralty courts, signaling tighter imperial control."}, {"q": "What made the Stamp Act of 1765 unique among earlier taxes?", "a": "It was Parliament's first direct tax on the colonies, requiring stamps on newspapers, legal documents, and printed goods."}, {"q": "Explain the colonial slogan 'no taxation without representation.'", "a": "Colonists argued only their own elected assemblies could tax them, since they had no representatives in Parliament."}, {"q": "What was 'virtual representation' and why did colonists reject it?", "a": "The British claim that every MP represented all subjects everywhere. Colonists rejected it, insisting only elected local assemblies could tax them."}, {"q": "Who were the Sons of Liberty?", "a": "Radical groups, strong in Boston and New York, that coordinated protests, intimidated stamp distributors, and enforced boycotts of British goods."}, {"q": "Why did Parliament pass the Declaratory Act in 1766?", "a": "After repealing the Stamp Act, it asserted Parliament could still legislate for the colonies 'in all cases whatsoever,' preserving its claimed authority."}, {"q": "How did colonists respond to the Townshend duties of 1767?", "a": "They revived nonimportation; homespun cloth and the Daughters of Liberty became symbols of colonial virtue and resistance."}, {"q": "What happened in the Boston Massacre of 1770?", "a": "British soldiers fired into a crowd, killing five including Crispus Attucks. Paul Revere's engraving turned it into anti-British propaganda."}, {"q": "What did the Boston Tea Party protest, and how?", "a": "In 1773 colonists disguised as Indians dumped over 300 chests of East India Company tea into the harbor, rejecting Parliament's power to tax."}, {"q": "Why did the Intolerable Acts unite the colonies?", "a": "Britain's harsh punishment of Massachusetts (closing Boston's port, gutting its charter) alarmed all colonies that their liberties could be next."}, {"q": "What did the First Continental Congress accomplish in 1774?", "a": "It organized a trade embargo enforced by local committees, drawing ordinary farmers and artisans into political life."}, {"q": "Where and when did the Revolutionary War begin?", "a": "At Lexington and Concord in April 1775, when British troops marched to seize colonial arms and were met by militia."}, {"q": "How did Thomas Paine's Common Sense change the debate in 1776?", "a": "Its plain, popular attack on monarchy demolished the case for reconciliation and urged immediate independence as a worldwide cause."}, {"q": "On what basis did the Declaration of Independence justify the break with Britain?", "a": "On universal natural rights and consent of the governed, not merely the traditional rights of Englishmen."}, {"q": "Who wrote the Declaration of Independence and what rights did it proclaim?", "a": "Thomas Jefferson, who proclaimed unalienable rights to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness, resting government on popular consent."}, {"q": "What was Lord Dunmore's Proclamation of 1775?", "a": "The Virginia royal governor's offer of freedom to enslaved people who fled to fight for Britain, pushing southern slaveholders toward independence."}, {"q": "Why was the Battle of Saratoga a turning point?", "a": "The 1777 American victory forced Burgoyne's surrender and convinced France the Americans could win, leading to the 1778 alliance."}, {"q": "How did the French alliance change the war?", "a": "France's 1778 entry turned a colonial rebellion into a global war, providing troops, ships, and money critical to American victory."}, {"q": "What was Washington's basic military strategy?", "a": "Avoid decisive defeat and keep his army in the field, surviving hardships like Valley Forge while landing morale-savers at Trenton and Princeton."}, {"q": "Why were the battles of Trenton and Princeton significant?", "a": "These late-1776 victories restored American morale after a string of defeats and kept the cause alive through a bleak winter."}, {"q": "What ended major fighting in the Revolution?", "a": "Cornwallis's surrender at Yorktown in 1781, trapped by American forces and the French fleet, effectively ended British hopes of victory."}, {"q": "What did the Treaty of Paris (1783) grant the United States?", "a": "Recognition of independence and borders stretching from Canada to Florida and west to the Mississippi River."}, {"q": "Compare the rights colonists claimed early vs. late in the crisis.", "a": "Early on they claimed the traditional rights of Englishmen; by 1776, via Common Sense and the Declaration, they asserted universal natural rights."}, {"q": "How did resistance tactics expand political participation?", "a": "Boycotts, committees, and crowd actions pulled artisans, small farmers, and women into political life, broadening popular involvement."}, {"q": "What role did enslaved people play during the Revolution?", "a": "Thousands sought freedom by serving either side; Dunmore's proclamation and Black service linked liberty rhetoric to emancipation."}, {"q": "Who were the Committees of Correspondence?", "a": "Networks of organizers, promoted by Samuel Adams, that spread news of British actions among colonies and knit resistance together."}, {"q": "Why did the Stamp Act Congress matter?", "a": "It was an early show of intercolonial unity, coordinating protest against the Stamp Act and helping force its 1766 repeal."}, {"q": "How did economic choices become political weapons against Britain?", "a": "Nonimportation agreements, boycotts, and homespun production turned consumption into acts of patriotic resistance."}, {"q": "Why did the Declaration of Independence have lasting global impact?", "a": "Its assertion of universal natural rights became a template inspiring later independence and anticolonial movements worldwide."}], "6": [{"q": "What broader contest did independence set off beyond the war with Britain?", "a": "A struggle over what kind of society America would become, as the claim that all men are created equal challenged colonial hierarchies of deference."}, {"q": "Whom did the ideal of deference require colonists to defer to?", "a": "Rulers, husbands, masters, and landlords. Revolutionary equality challenged all of these inherited hierarchies."}, {"q": "Why was Pennsylvania's 1776 constitution the most democratic?", "a": "It created a single-house assembly elected annually by all taxpaying men and abolished the governorship, dropping property qualifications."}, {"q": "What was John Adams's Thoughts on Government, and its influence?", "a": "A 1776 blueprint urging balanced two-house legislatures and strong executives; most states followed it rather than Pennsylvania's radical model."}, {"q": "Define a republic as understood in the revolutionary era.", "a": "A government resting on the consent of the governed, with elected representatives and no king or hereditary aristocracy."}, {"q": "How did suffrage change in the new states after independence?", "a": "Most states lowered or eliminated property requirements, so by the 1780s the vast majority of adult white men could vote."}, {"q": "What did disestablishment accomplish?", "a": "States stripped official churches of public funding and legal privileges, institutionalizing religious liberty."}, {"q": "What did Jefferson's Virginia Bill for Establishing Religious Freedom do?", "a": "Enacted in 1786, it ended religious tests for office and state funding of churches, making religious liberty a model American right."}, {"q": "What was Christian Republicanism?", "a": "A blend of evangelical and republican thought holding that religion and self-discipline were needed to restrain vice and sustain virtuous citizens."}, {"q": "How did the decline of indentured servitude reshape labor?", "a": "As servitude and apprenticeship faded, free wage labor defined the North, sharpening its contrast with southern slavery."}, {"q": "What economic vision did Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations offer?", "a": "That the free market's 'invisible hand' organized economic life better than regulation, arming American opponents of price controls."}, {"q": "Contrast the two competing economic visions revealed by wartime inflation.", "a": "An older ideal of a community-regulated economy with price controls versus Adam Smith's new free-market vision of unregulated trade."}, {"q": "Who were the Loyalists and how many left the country?", "a": "The roughly 20-25% of free Americans who sided with Britain. Facing oaths, confiscation, and exile, about 60,000 left the United States."}, {"q": "How did the Treaty of Paris affect Native Americans?", "a": "It ignored their land claims, whether they had allied with Britain or the patriots, opening their lands to white settlement."}, {"q": "What were freedom petitions?", "a": "Appeals by enslaved New Englanders to courts and legislatures in the 1770s using the Revolution's own natural-rights language to demand emancipation."}, {"q": "Who was Lemuel Haynes and what did he argue?", "a": "A Black Massachusetts militiaman and later minister who argued in 1776 that if liberty was an innate right, Africans deserved it equally."}, {"q": "How did gradual abolition work in the North?", "a": "Between 1777 and 1804 northern states freed children born to enslaved mothers only after years of service, so slavery died out slowly."}, {"q": "How did the free Black population change from 1776 to 1810?", "a": "It grew from about 10,000 to nearly 200,000, building independent churches, schools, and communities."}, {"q": "What was coverture and did the Revolution change it?", "a": "The doctrine that a married woman's legal identity was absorbed into her husband's; it survived the Revolution intact."}, {"q": "Define republican motherhood.", "a": "The postwar ideal that women should raise virtuous future citizens, justifying expanded female education while still barring women from politics."}, {"q": "Why did anti-Catholic prejudice soften after 1778?", "a": "The alliance with Catholic France reduced hostility, and denominations multiplied as religious pluralism grew."}, {"q": "What did Abigail Adams urge, and what was the result?", "a": "She pressed for recognition of women's interests ('remember the ladies'), but women remained excluded from formal politics by coverture."}, {"q": "Compare the northern and southern labor systems after the Revolution.", "a": "The North became a free-labor society as servitude declined, while the South kept and expanded slavery, deepening the sectional divide."}, {"q": "How did enslaved people turn revolutionary ideology into a weapon?", "a": "Through freedom petitions, wartime escape, and military service, they demanded liberty on the same natural-rights grounds patriots invoked."}, {"q": "Did slavery shrink overall because of the Revolution?", "a": "No. The 1790 census counted 700,000 enslaved people; despite northern abolition, slavery kept growing in the South."}, {"q": "What was significant about Vermont's 1777 constitution?", "a": "It became the first to ban slavery, an early sign that revolutionary ideals could translate into emancipation."}, {"q": "Who was Deborah Sampson?", "a": "A woman who enlisted in the Continental army in 1782 disguised as a man, illustrating women's unconventional wartime participation."}, {"q": "What did Samuel Sewall's The Selling of Joseph represent?", "a": "Published in 1700, it was America's first antislavery tract, an early precedent for later abolitionist arguments."}, {"q": "Why did state constitutions shift power to legislatures?", "a": "Reflecting distrust of executive and monarchical authority, they placed power in elected assemblies to embody the consent of the governed."}, {"q": "How did the Revolution reshape the boundaries of who counted as free?", "a": "Competing claims by women, African Americans, Loyalists, and Native Americans exposed sharp limits on who was included as a free citizen."}], "7": [{"q": "What were the Articles of Confederation and their key weaknesses?", "a": "America's first constitution (1781-1788): a one-house Congress with no president, no courts, and no power to tax or regulate trade."}, {"q": "Why did the Articles create such a weak central government?", "a": "They reflected revolutionary fears of concentrated power, leaving sovereignty largely with the states after the fight against British authority."}, {"q": "What lasting achievement came from the Confederation era?", "a": "The land ordinances of 1784, 1785, and 1787, which set up survey and sale of the West and promised new equal states."}, {"q": "What did the Northwest Ordinance of 1787 provide?", "a": "It organized territory north of the Ohio, promised three to five future equal states, pledged fair treatment of Indians, and banned slavery there."}, {"q": "Explain Jefferson's 'empire of liberty.'", "a": "The vision that the U.S. would expand by admitting western territories as self-governing equal states rather than ruling them as colonies."}, {"q": "What was Shays's Rebellion and why did it matter?", "a": "A 1786-87 uprising of indebted Massachusetts farmers who closed courts; its suppression convinced leaders the national government must be strengthened."}, {"q": "Who convened at the Constitutional Convention and where?", "a": "Fifty-five delegates met in Philadelphia in 1787, presided over by Washington, and drafted a new Constitution in secret sessions."}, {"q": "Compare the Virginia and New Jersey Plans.", "a": "The Virginia Plan (Madison) wanted a bicameral legislature by population, favoring large states; the New Jersey Plan wanted a one-house Congress with equal state votes."}, {"q": "What did the Great Compromise resolve?", "a": "It merged the plans into a Congress with a population-based House and a state-equal Senate, satisfying both large and small states."}, {"q": "Define federalism as set up by the Constitution.", "a": "The division of authority between national and state governments, leaving most daily governance to states while strengthening central power over taxes, commerce, and war."}, {"q": "How do checks and balances prevent tyranny?", "a": "Power is split among legislative, executive, and judicial branches with tools like the veto, impeachment, and appointments so no branch dominates."}, {"q": "What was the three-fifths clause and its effect?", "a": "It counted three-fifths of enslaved people toward House seats and electoral votes, giving slave states outsized national power for decades."}, {"q": "How did the Constitution treat the Atlantic slave trade?", "a": "It protected the trade from federal ban until 1808, and a fugitive slave clause extended bondage across state lines."}, {"q": "Why is it notable the word 'slavery' never appears in the Constitution?", "a": "Delegates protected slavery through euphemisms in the three-fifths, slave trade, and fugitive clauses without naming it, masking the compromise."}, {"q": "Who were the Federalists in the ratification debate?", "a": "Supporters of the Constitution, backed by urban commercial interests and armed with The Federalist essays, who wanted a stronger national government."}, {"q": "Who were the Anti-Federalists and what did they fear?", "a": "Opponents like Patrick Henry and small farmers who feared distant, aristocratic rule; their pressure produced the Bill of Rights."}, {"q": "What was The Federalist and who wrote it?", "a": "Eighty-five pro-ratification essays by Hamilton, Madison, and Jay as 'Publius'; numbers 10 and 51 argued a large republic would check factions."}, {"q": "What argument did Madison make in Federalist No. 10?", "a": "That a large, diverse republic would prevent any single faction from oppressing the rest, inverting the idea that republics must be small."}, {"q": "When was the Bill of Rights ratified and what did it protect?", "a": "In 1791. It guaranteed freedoms of religion, speech, press, and assembly and fair legal procedures against the federal government."}, {"q": "How did Madison's promise of a Bill of Rights affect ratification?", "a": "It won over wavering supporters and defused Anti-Federalist fears of unchecked federal power, sealing the Constitution's ratification victory."}, {"q": "What was Little Turtle's significance in 1791?", "a": "The Miami leader routed St. Clair's army in the Ohio Valley, a major Native victory before later defeat at Fallen Timbers."}, {"q": "What did the Battle of Fallen Timbers and Treaty of Greenville accomplish?", "a": "The 1794 U.S. victory led to the 1795 treaty in which twelve tribes ceded most of Ohio and Indiana and accepted annuity payments."}, {"q": "What did the Naturalization Act of 1790 do?", "a": "It restricted naturalized citizenship to 'free white persons,' racially bounding who could become an American for the next eighty years."}, {"q": "How did Crevecoeur's 'melting pot' reveal exclusion?", "a": "His famous image of blended Americans imagined only Europeans, leaving out Native Americans and African Americans."}, {"q": "What did Jefferson argue in Notes on the State of Virginia?", "a": "That Black Americans could not be incorporated as equal citizens, giving intellectual cover to a hardening racial ideology."}, {"q": "How were Native Americans treated under the new constitutional order?", "a": "As separate foreign nations whose land was taken by treaty and war, excluded from 'We the People.'"}, {"q": "What did the Land Ordinance of 1785 establish?", "a": "A rectangular survey and sale system for the public domain in the Old Northwest, imposing order on western settlement."}, {"q": "How did postwar economic troubles push toward a new constitution?", "a": "Depression, unpayable war debts, and state paper-money schemes alarmed nationalists who feared for property rights and national credit."}, {"q": "Why did the phrase 'We the People' conceal exclusions?", "a": "Despite its inclusive sound, Indians were treated as foreign nations, most Blacks were enslaved, and citizenship was reserved for whites."}, {"q": "When did Congress finally ban slave importation, and why then?", "a": "In 1808, the earliest date the Constitution's slave-trade clause permitted federal prohibition."}], "8": [{"q": "What five parts made up Hamilton's financial program?", "a": "Funding the war debt at face value, assuming state debts, a national bank, a whiskey excise, and encouraging manufacturing."}, {"q": "What kind of nation did Hamilton's program aim to build?", "a": "A powerful commercial republic, closely tied to Britain, with wealthy creditors bound to the new federal government."}, {"q": "Why did Jefferson and Madison oppose Hamilton's plan?", "a": "They envisioned an agrarian nation expanding westward and saw the plan as corrupt favoritism toward speculators, becoming strict constructionists."}, {"q": "What is strict construction of the Constitution?", "a": "The view that the federal government may exercise only powers explicitly granted, used by Jefferson and Madison against the national bank."}, {"q": "What was the Whiskey Rebellion of 1794?", "a": "Western Pennsylvania farmers resisted the federal excise on distilled spirits; Washington personally led about 13,000 militiamen to suppress it."}, {"q": "Why did Jay's Treaty (1794) anger Republicans?", "a": "Britain gave up northwestern forts but conceded nothing on impressment or neutral shipping; its pro-British tilt crystallized the Republican opposition."}, {"q": "Contrast the Federalist and Republican parties.", "a": "Federalists (Washington, Adams, Hamilton) backed strong central government and Britain; Republicans (Jefferson, Madison) backed states' rights, farmers, and France."}, {"q": "What was the XYZ affair?", "a": "A 1798 scandal in which French agents demanded bribes from American envoys, triggering an undeclared naval quasi-war with France."}, {"q": "What did the Alien and Sedition Acts of 1798 do?", "a": "They lengthened naturalization, allowed deportation of 'dangerous' foreigners, and criminalized criticism of the government, targeting Republican editors."}, {"q": "What were the Virginia and Kentucky Resolutions?", "a": "Protests by Madison and Jefferson declaring the Sedition Act unconstitutional; Jefferson's suggested states could nullify federal law."}, {"q": "Why is the election of 1800 called the 'Revolution of 1800'?", "a": "Jefferson's defeat of Adams marked the first peaceful transfer of power between rival American parties."}, {"q": "How did the three-fifths clause affect the 1800 election?", "a": "It inflated southern electoral votes, tipping the outcome to Jefferson over Adams."}, {"q": "What constitutional change came from the 1800 electoral tie with Burr?", "a": "The Twelfth Amendment, requiring separate electoral ballots for president and vice president."}, {"q": "What was the Haitian Revolution and its dual effect on Americans?", "a": "Toussaint L'Ouverture's slave uprising created independent Haiti (1804), inspiring enslaved Americans while terrifying white slaveholders."}, {"q": "What was Gabriel's Rebellion and its aftermath?", "a": "A planned 1800 march on Richmond by enslaved Virginians; its discovery brought executions and laws restricting free Blacks and manumission."}, {"q": "What did Marbury v. Madison (1803) establish?", "a": "The Marshall Court's power of judicial review, allowing the Supreme Court to strike down acts of Congress as unconstitutional."}, {"q": "What was the Louisiana Purchase and its significance?", "a": "Jefferson's 1803 $15 million buy from Napoleon of land between the Mississippi and Rockies, doubling the nation's size."}, {"q": "Why did the Louisiana Purchase strain Jefferson's principles?", "a": "As a strict constructionist he doubted the Constitution authorized land purchases, but he bent his scruples to seize the opportunity."}, {"q": "How did the Haitian Revolution enable the Louisiana Purchase?", "a": "Haiti's defeat of French forces led Napoleon to abandon his American empire and sell Louisiana to the United States."}, {"q": "What did the Lewis and Clark expedition do?", "a": "Departing in 1804, it mapped and explored the newly acquired West, opening the path for future expansion."}, {"q": "What was the Embargo Act of 1807 and did it work?", "a": "Jefferson's ban on American ships sailing to foreign ports to pressure Britain and France; it slashed exports 80% and hurt U.S. ports without changing European policy."}, {"q": "Who were Tecumseh and Tenskwatawa?", "a": "Shawnee brothers who led a pan-Indian movement against land cessions; Tenskwatawa preached cultural revival, Tecumseh built a confederacy."}, {"q": "Who were the War Hawks?", "a": "Young nationalist congressmen like Henry Clay and John C. Calhoun who pushed for war with Britain in 1812."}, {"q": "What caused the War of 1812?", "a": "British impressment of sailors, seizures of ships, and support for Indian resistance drove a divided nation, led by Madison, into war."}, {"q": "What happened at the Battle of the Thames in 1813?", "a": "American forces defeated the British and their Native allies, and Tecumseh was killed, breaking his confederacy."}, {"q": "What was Andrew Jackson's role in the war?", "a": "He crushed the Creeks at Horseshoe Bend and won the Battle of New Orleans in 1815, becoming a national hero."}, {"q": "What did the Treaty of Ghent settle?", "a": "It ended the War of 1812 with no territorial change, restoring the prewar status quo despite the conflict."}, {"q": "What was the Hartford Convention and its consequence?", "a": "A meeting of antiwar New England Federalists asserting state 'interposition'; overtaken by peace and New Orleans, it doomed the Federalist Party."}, {"q": "Why is the War of 1812 called the 'Second War of Independence'?", "a": "It confirmed U.S. independence, broke Indian power east of the Mississippi, discredited Federalists, and cemented American nationalism."}, {"q": "How did the Battle of Fort McHenry gain lasting symbolic meaning?", "a": "Its 1814 defense inspired the Star-Spangled Banner, becoming a lasting emblem of American nationalism."}], "9": [{"q": "What was the market revolution?", "a": "The early 1800s shift in which new transportation, communication, and production drew Americans out of local self-sufficiency into buying and selling in national and world markets."}, {"q": "Who proved the steamboat commercially practical, and when?", "a": "Robert Fulton, whose Clermont steamed up the Hudson River in 1807, opening upstream river commerce."}, {"q": "Why did the Erie Canal make New York City the nation's leading port?", "a": "Finished in 1825, the state-funded 363-mile canal linked the Great Lakes to the Hudson, funneling western trade through NYC and cutting shipping costs."}, {"q": "How did the cotton gin reshape the South?", "a": "Whitney's 1793 machine quickly removed seeds from short-staple cotton, making it hugely profitable and driving slavery's westward expansion into the Cotton Kingdom."}, {"q": "What was the Cotton Kingdom?", "a": "The slave-labor cotton belt that spread across the Deep South after the War of 1812, supplying most of the world's cotton to northern and European mills."}, {"q": "How did closing the Atlantic slave trade affect the internal slave trade?", "a": "It spurred a massive domestic trade that moved roughly a million enslaved people from the Upper South to the Deep South, shattering families."}, {"q": "Who were the Lowell mill girls?", "a": "Young unmarried New England farm women recruited to tend textile machines, living in supervised boardinghouses and among the first American women to earn independent wages."}, {"q": "What was the American system of manufactures?", "a": "A production method using interchangeable, mass-produced parts assembled into standardized goods like clocks and firearms, spreading mechanical skill across the North."}, {"q": "Why did nativism rise in the 1830s-1850s?", "a": "Mass Irish Catholic and German immigration provoked hostility from those who blamed newcomers for crime, corruption, and falling wages, sparking urban riots."}, {"q": "What drove huge Irish immigration in the late 1840s?", "a": "Ireland's Great Famine (1845-1851) forced roughly a million people to emigrate, most of them to the United States."}, {"q": "What did the Second Great Awakening emphasize?", "a": "A wave of Protestant revivals stressing free moral agency and universal salvation, making Methodists and Baptists the largest denominations."}, {"q": "Who led revivals emphasizing free moral agency in the early 1800s?", "a": "Charles G. Finney, a leading preacher of the Second Great Awakening who stressed that individuals could choose salvation and moral reform."}, {"q": "What did the transcendentalists believe?", "a": "New England thinkers like Emerson and Thoreau prized individual judgment and self-reliance over social convention and criticized market-era materialism."}, {"q": "What is manifest destiny, and who named it?", "a": "The belief that the U.S. had a God-given right to expand across North America; journalist John O'Sullivan coined the phrase in 1845."}, {"q": "How did Mormonism grow out of the revival era?", "a": "Joseph Smith founded the church in upstate New York's revival country; persecution led to his 1844 murder and Brigham Young's migration to Utah."}, {"q": "What was the cult of domesticity?", "a": "The middle-class ideal that a woman's proper role was creating a virtuous home apart from the competitive market, limiting her public and economic life."}, {"q": "What did Gibbons v. Ogden (1824) establish?", "a": "The Marshall Court struck down New York's steamboat monopoly, confirming federal authority over interstate commerce."}, {"q": "Why did Dartmouth College v. Woodward (1819) matter for business?", "a": "It held that a corporate charter is a contract states cannot alter, shielding corporations from state interference and encouraging their growth."}, {"q": "What was the 'family wage' idea?", "a": "The belief that a male breadwinner should earn enough to support his whole household, defining paid labor as men's sphere and devaluing women's work."}, {"q": "Compare how the market revolution developed in the North versus the South.", "a": "The North built commercial farms, cities, and factories with wage labor and immigrants; the South relied on slave-grown cotton and stayed rural."}, {"q": "Why did canals and railroads matter for the national economy?", "a": "They dramatically lowered the cost of moving goods, people, and information, integrating once-isolated local economies into a single national market."}, {"q": "What did Cyrus McCormick invent, and when?", "a": "The mechanical reaper, in 1831, which sped up wheat harvesting and boosted commercial grain farming in the West."}, {"q": "What did John Deere contribute to western farming?", "a": "He introduced the steel plow in 1837, letting farmers cut through the tough, root-filled prairie soils of the Midwest."}, {"q": "How did the telegraph change communication?", "a": "Samuel Morse's telegraph entered commercial service in 1844, enabling near-instant long-distance communication for the first time."}, {"q": "How were the benefits of the market revolution distributed unevenly?", "a": "White men's opportunities expanded while free African Americans faced exclusion, women were confined by domesticity, and many wage workers lost independence."}, {"q": "Why did early unions and Workingmen's Parties form?", "a": "Wage workers, hit by the Panics of 1819 and 1837, organized to defend the older ideal of economic independence against factory wage labor."}, {"q": "What territory did the U.S. gain in 1819, and how?", "a": "Florida, transferred from Spain through the Adams-Onis Treaty."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the 1814 Waltham factory?", "a": "The Boston Associates' Waltham mill combined all stages of cloth production under one roof, a model for the American factory system."}, {"q": "How did Thoreau critique market society in Walden (1854)?", "a": "He argued that materialism and endless getting corrupted true living, urging simplicity, self-reliance, and closeness to nature instead."}, {"q": "How did the market revolution redefine American freedom?", "a": "Freedom increasingly meant individual self-improvement, mobility, and economic opportunity, though defined along lines of race and gender."}], "10": [{"q": "How democratic had white male suffrage become by 1840?", "a": "States had dropped property requirements, so over 90 percent of adult white men could vote, and a cheap penny press informed a mass electorate."}, {"q": "How did democratization coincide with new exclusions?", "a": "As white men gained the vote, women stayed shut out and state after state stripped free Black men of suffrage, making race the boundary of citizenship."}, {"q": "Who described American democracy as a whole culture of equality?", "a": "Alexis de Tocqueville, a French visitor of the early 1830s whose observations became Democracy in America."}, {"q": "What was the Dorr War of 1841?", "a": "A Rhode Island uprising against strict property-based voting rules, showing how explosive even white male disfranchisement had become."}, {"q": "What were the three parts of Henry Clay's American System?", "a": "A national bank, protective tariffs, and federally funded internal improvements like roads and canals, meant to knit the sections together economically."}, {"q": "What did the Monroe Doctrine declare in 1823?", "a": "Drafted by John Quincy Adams, it warned Europe against new colonization in the Americas and pledged U.S. non-involvement in European wars."}, {"q": "What caused the Panic of 1819?", "a": "Falling crop prices, shrinking European demand, and reckless land speculation, which deepened popular distrust of banks, especially the Second Bank."}, {"q": "What did McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) decide?", "a": "The Marshall Court ruled states could not tax the national bank, endorsing a broad 'necessary and proper' reading of congressional power."}, {"q": "How did the Missouri Compromise (1820) settle slavery's expansion?", "a": "It admitted Missouri as a slave state and Maine as free, and banned slavery in Louisiana Purchase land north of the 36 degrees 30 minutes line."}, {"q": "Why did the Missouri debate matter beyond 1820?", "a": "It exposed how explosive the question of slavery's westward expansion was, previewing the sectional conflicts that would follow."}, {"q": "What was the 'corrupt bargain' of 1825?", "a": "The charge that Clay swung House support to make John Quincy Adams president in exchange for becoming secretary of state, energizing Jackson's 1828 run."}, {"q": "What was the Era of Good Feelings?", "a": "The label for Monroe's presidency (1817-1825), when the Federalists collapsed and apparent one-party unity masked growing sectional rivalries."}, {"q": "What was the spoils system?", "a": "Jackson's practice of rotating government jobs among loyal party supporters, treating officeholding as a democratic reward, not a permanent bureaucracy."}, {"q": "What was the 'tariff of abominations'?", "a": "The steep 1828 tariff that southerners, especially South Carolinians, denounced as enriching northern industry at the South's expense."}, {"q": "What triggered the nullification crisis, and how did it end?", "a": "South Carolina tried to void the tariff in 1832 using Calhoun's theory; Jackson answered with the Force Act before a compromise tariff ended it in 1833."}, {"q": "How did Calhoun's and Jackson's constitutional views clash?", "a": "Calhoun's compact theory held states could nullify federal laws, while Jackson insisted on federal supremacy, previewing the argument over secession."}, {"q": "Who was John C. Calhoun?", "a": "The South Carolina vice president and theorist who shifted from nationalism to states' rights and secretly wrote the Exposition and Protest defending nullification."}, {"q": "What did the Indian Removal Act (1830) do?", "a": "It funded treaties to relocate the Five Civilized Tribes from the Southeast to land west of the Mississippi, repudiating assimilation."}, {"q": "What did Worcester v. Georgia (1832) rule, and how did Jackson respond?", "a": "The Court recognized Indian nations as distinct political communities beyond state jurisdiction, but Jackson refused to enforce the decision."}, {"q": "What was the Trail of Tears?", "a": "The army's forced 1838-1839 march of about 18,000 Cherokee to present-day Oklahoma, during which roughly a quarter died."}, {"q": "What was the Bank War?", "a": "Jackson's fight against Nicholas Biddle's Second Bank; his 1832 veto of recharter, framed as defending ordinary citizens, killed the bank."}, {"q": "How did the Bank War expand presidential power?", "a": "Jackson used the veto aggressively and appealed directly to voters, asserting the president as the people's tribune against privilege."}, {"q": "What caused the Panic of 1837?", "a": "Speculative excess, the Specie Circular, and tightening British credit set off a collapse and a depression lasting into the 1840s."}, {"q": "How did the Panic of 1837 affect Van Buren's presidency?", "a": "The long depression sank his popularity and helped the Whigs defeat him in 1840."}, {"q": "Who built the Democratic Party machinery behind Jackson?", "a": "Martin Van Buren, whose organizing helped elect Jackson in 1828 and made mass-based parties central to politics."}, {"q": "What was the second party system?", "a": "The competition between Democrats and Whigs that made permanent, mass-based national parties a fixture of American government."}, {"q": "What was notable about the 1840 'log cabin' campaign?", "a": "Whig William Henry Harrison won using marketplace-style advertising and mass appeal, with turnout reaching about 80 percent."}, {"q": "How did the Monroe Doctrine assert a U.S. hemispheric role?", "a": "By declaring the Americas closed to European colonization, it claimed U.S. leadership in the Western Hemisphere despite limited power to enforce it."}, {"q": "What contradictions did Jackson embody?", "a": "A self-made champion of the common white man who defended states' rights, forced Indian removal, and destroyed the national bank as privilege."}, {"q": "How did the market revolution shape party conflict?", "a": "Debates over banks, currency, tariffs, and internal improvements divided pro-development Whigs from Democrats wary of concentrated economic power."}], "11": [{"q": "Why was slavery called the South's 'peculiar institution'?", "a": "The euphemism marked slavery as unique to the South after northern abolition, framing it as the region's distinctive social system."}, {"q": "How central was cotton to the U.S. economy by 1860?", "a": "Southern cotton supplied about three-quarters of the world's raw cotton and made up more than half of U.S. export earnings."}, {"q": "What was the Second Middle Passage?", "a": "The internal slave trade (roughly 1820-1860) that shipped over 2 million people from the Upper South to the cotton and sugar frontier, breaking up families."}, {"q": "How did closing the Atlantic slave trade in 1808 affect the South?", "a": "It ended legal importation, spurring the domestic trade that relocated more than 2 million enslaved people to the Deep South."}, {"q": "What did James Henry Hammond mean by 'Cotton Is King'?", "a": "His boast that the world economy's dependence on southern cotton made the slave system too powerful and profitable to be politically challenged."}, {"q": "What was paternalism as slaveholders defined it?", "a": "The self-justifying ideology that masters were caring father figures, an image contradicted by sales, whippings, and exploitation."}, {"q": "How did the proslavery argument change after 1830?", "a": "It shifted from calling slavery a necessary evil to defending it as a positive good, resting on racism, the Bible, and claims it guaranteed white equality."}, {"q": "Who argued slavery, not liberty, was the basis of civilization?", "a": "George Fitzhugh, a Virginia writer who pushed proslavery thought to its extreme against the Declaration's universal-liberty language."}, {"q": "Who were the 'plain folk,' and why did they support slavery?", "a": "The non-slaveholding white majority; racism, kinship ties, and belief that slavery protected their independence bound them to the planter class."}, {"q": "Contrast gang labor with the task system.", "a": "Gang labor meant supervised dawn-to-dusk field work in cotton and sugar; the task system, common on rice plantations, assigned daily jobs that left some free time."}, {"q": "What made slave religion distinctive?", "a": "It blended African traditions with biblical themes, especially the Exodus story, to affirm enslaved people's worth and promise eventual deliverance."}, {"q": "How did the law define enslaved people?", "a": "As property: they could not testify against whites, own guns, move freely, or (after the 1830s) legally learn to read, with discipline resting on the whip and sale."}, {"q": "Who were most successful fugitive slaves?", "a": "Usually young men from the Upper South; others hid in swamps, cities, or Native communities rather than reaching the free states."}, {"q": "What was the Underground Railroad?", "a": "An informal network of abolitionists and safe houses that helped guide escaped slaves northward to freedom before the Civil War."}, {"q": "Who was Harriet Tubman?", "a": "A Maryland-born woman who escaped slavery in 1849 and repeatedly returned south, leading dozens of enslaved people to freedom at great personal risk."}, {"q": "What happened aboard the Amistad, and what was the result?", "a": "Enslaved Africans seized the ship in 1839; John Quincy Adams argued their case, and the Supreme Court freed them as illegally trafficked."}, {"q": "What was Denmark Vesey's conspiracy?", "a": "An alleged 1822 Charleston uprising organized by a free Black carpenter who drew on the Bible, the Declaration, and Haiti; it was betrayed before it began."}, {"q": "What was Nat Turner's Rebellion, and why did it matter?", "a": "An 1831 Virginia revolt led by a slave preacher that killed about sixty whites; it terrified the South and produced harsher slave codes, not emancipation."}, {"q": "What did the case of Celia (1855) reveal about slave law?", "a": "The Missouri court ruled self-defense laws protecting 'any woman' did not apply to a slave who killed her rapist master, exposing slaves' lack of legal personhood."}, {"q": "How did the Haitian Revolution shape American slavery?", "a": "The 1791-1804 overthrow of slavery in Saint-Domingue inspired hope among enslaved Americans and deep fear among slaveholders."}, {"q": "What was Gabriel's rebellion?", "a": "A large 1800 slave uprising planned near Richmond, Virginia, that was uncovered and suppressed before it could begin."}, {"q": "What was the 1811 Louisiana uprising?", "a": "The era's largest slave revolt, in which enslaved sugar-plantation workers marched toward New Orleans before being crushed."}, {"q": "How did British emancipation in 1833 affect the American South?", "a": "By abolishing slavery throughout its empire, Britain isolated the South in a Western world where slavery was in retreat."}, {"q": "Who escaped slavery in Maryland in 1838 and became a leading abolitionist?", "a": "Frederick Douglass, who fled north in 1838 and became a powerful voice against slavery."}, {"q": "How did northern businesses profit from slavery?", "a": "Northern banks, ships, insurers, and textile mills all made money financing, transporting, and processing slave-grown cotton, tying the whole nation to bondage."}, {"q": "Why did the South remain rural and under-industrialized?", "a": "Wealth was locked in land and enslaved labor and controlled by a small planter elite, leaving little investment in factories or cities."}, {"q": "How large was the enslaved population and its value by 1860?", "a": "Nearly 4 million enslaved people lived in the South, and their market value exceeded that of all U.S. factories, railroads, and banks combined."}, {"q": "How did enslaved communities preserve dignity under bondage?", "a": "They built a semi-autonomous culture of family ties, religion, and folklore that sustained resistance and kept alive hope for freedom."}, {"q": "How did Virginia respond to Nat Turner's revolt in 1832?", "a": "It debated but rejected gradual emancipation and instead tightened its slave codes and suppressed antislavery dissent."}, {"q": "What forms did slave resistance take?", "a": "It ranged from everyday sabotage and running away to shipboard seizures and armed revolts like those of Vesey and Nat Turner."}], "12": [{"q": "What idea from the Second Great Awakening fueled antebellum reform?", "a": "The belief that people are free moral agents, which convinced reformers that sin and social ills could be eradicated, not merely managed."}, {"q": "What was perfectionism?", "a": "The revival-era conviction that individuals and society were capable of unlimited moral improvement, underlying nearly every antebellum reform crusade."}, {"q": "What were utopian communities?", "a": "Roughly a hundred experimental settlements founded before the Civil War that reorganized property, labor, and family on cooperative lines to model a better society."}, {"q": "What did the Shakers believe and practice?", "a": "The most successful communal sect believed in God's dual male-female nature and practiced celibacy, building orderly, prosperous villages from Maine to Kentucky."}, {"q": "What was New Harmony?", "a": "Robert Owen's secular cooperative community in Indiana (1825); it collapsed quickly but influenced labor, education, and women's rights reformers."}, {"q": "What made Oneida controversial?", "a": "John Humphrey Noyes's upstate New York community (1848) practiced 'complex marriage' and demanded strict obedience to his religious teachings."}, {"q": "How did the temperance movement change over time?", "a": "It grew from urging moderation into demanding total abstinence, and by 1840 had helped cut per-capita drinking by more than half."}, {"q": "What did Horace Mann champion, and why?", "a": "Tax-supported common schools to mix children of all classes and prepare citizens for self-government; by 1860 every northern state had them."}, {"q": "Why was reform weakest in the South?", "a": "Reform movements smelled of antislavery to white southerners, who saw them as a threat to slavery and the region's social order."}, {"q": "Who resented moral reform, and why?", "a": "Tavern-going workingmen and Catholic immigrants saw temperance and Sabbath laws as an assault on their personal liberty."}, {"q": "What was the American Colonization Society?", "a": "Founded in 1816 to pair gradual emancipation with resettling free Black Americans in Liberia; most African Americans rejected it as their home was America."}, {"q": "What did David Walker's Appeal (1829) demand?", "a": "The pamphlet by a free Black Bostonian condemned slavery and racism, urged Black resistance and pride, and warned of divine judgment on the nation."}, {"q": "Who was William Lloyd Garrison?", "a": "Editor of The Liberator (1831) and the leading voice of immediate abolition, who rejected colonization and even burned the Constitution as proslavery."}, {"q": "How did abolitionism change in the 1830s?", "a": "It shifted from gradual emancipation and colonization to demanding immediate abolition and equal Black citizenship, spread through mass print and moral suasion."}, {"q": "What was the American Anti-Slavery Society?", "a": "The national immediatist organization founded in 1833 that flooded the country with antislavery print and speakers, enrolling roughly 100,000 members."}, {"q": "What was moral suasion?", "a": "The abolitionist strategy of converting public opinion by persuading people of slavery's sinfulness, rather than using politics or violence."}, {"q": "How did Black abolitionists shape the movement?", "a": "Douglass, Henry Highland Garnet, and Sojourner Truth insisted on color-blind citizenship and exposed the hypocrisy of celebrating the Fourth of July amid slavery."}, {"q": "What was the gag rule, and why did it matter?", "a": "The 1836 House rule barring antislavery petitions; John Quincy Adams's fight against it, won in 1844, tied abolition to free-speech rights."}, {"q": "How did mob violence help the antislavery cause?", "a": "Attacks like the 1837 murder of editor Elijah Lovejoy convinced many northerners that slavery threatened white civil liberties too."}, {"q": "How did abolitionism incubate feminism?", "a": "Women attacked for public speaking, like Abby Kelley and the Grimke sisters, began defending their own rights alongside the enslaved person's."}, {"q": "Who were the Grimke sisters?", "a": "Angelina and Sarah Grimke, daughters of a South Carolina slaveholder turned abolitionist lecturers who defended women's equality against attacks on their speaking."}, {"q": "What did Sarah Grimke and Margaret Fuller write?", "a": "Sarah Grimke's Letters on the Equality of the Sexes and Fuller's Woman in the Nineteenth Century pressed the case for women's rights."}, {"q": "What happened at the Seneca Falls Convention (1848)?", "a": "Organized by Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott, it issued a Declaration of Sentiments demanding the vote and full equality for women."}, {"q": "Why was the Declaration of Sentiments modeled on the Declaration of Independence?", "a": "To claim the nation's founding ideals of equality for women and demand suffrage in language Americans already revered."}, {"q": "What split organized abolitionism in 1840?", "a": "The dispute over women's roles fractured the movement, prompting some to form the political Liberty Party."}, {"q": "What was the Liberty Party?", "a": "The abolitionist third party formed after the 1840 schism, running James G. Birney for president to bring antislavery into electoral politics."}, {"q": "How did reformers pioneer mass public-opinion politics?", "a": "Abolitionists used cheap print, petitions, and lecture circuits to shape opinion, while the backlash made civil liberties a national issue."}, {"q": "What was Freedom's Journal?", "a": "The first Black newspaper in the United States, begun in 1827, giving African Americans a voice against slavery and racism."}, {"q": "What two 1852 works advanced the antislavery cause?", "a": "Harriet Beecher Stowe's Uncle Tom's Cabin was published, and Douglass delivered 'What, to the Slave, Is the Fourth of July?'"}, {"q": "How was antebellum reform transatlantic?", "a": "Temperance, peace, antislavery, and feminist activists exchanged ideas across the Atlantic and drew on models like British emancipation."}], "13": [{"q": "What was manifest destiny?", "a": "The belief that the U.S. was destined, even divinely ordained, to expand across North America to the Pacific, driving 1840s expansionism."}, {"q": "How did manifest destiny connect expansion to ideas about race?", "a": "It fused a democratic mission with beliefs in Anglo-Saxon racial superiority, framing expansion as spreading a supposedly superior civilization."}, {"q": "When did American settlers in Texas win independence from Mexico?", "a": "In 1836, after the fall of the Alamo and the decisive victory at San Jacinto, creating an independent slaveholding republic."}, {"q": "What triggered the Mexican War (1846-1848)?", "a": "The 1845 annexation of Texas plus disputes over its border and President Polk's desire for California provoked war with Mexico."}, {"q": "What did the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo accomplish?", "a": "It ended the Mexican War in 1848, transferring the Mexican Cession—over half a million square miles including California—to the U.S."}, {"q": "Why did the 1848 gold discovery in California matter?", "a": "It sparked a mass migration of hundreds of thousands and led to violent dispossession of California's Indians and foreign-born miners."}, {"q": "What was the Wilmot Proviso and why was it significant?", "a": "An 1846 proposal to ban slavery in land taken from Mexico; it passed the House by section rather than party, exposing sectional splits."}, {"q": "What did the Free Soil Party of 1848 reveal about antislavery politics?", "a": "Its strong showing under Van Buren proved opposition to slavery's spread had broad northern appeal beyond just abolitionists."}, {"q": "What were the main terms of the Compromise of 1850?", "a": "It admitted California free, ended D.C.'s slave trade, left Utah and New Mexico to popular sovereignty, and toughened the fugitive slave law."}, {"q": "Why did the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 outrage northerners?", "a": "It forced citizens to help return runaways without jury trials, nationalizing slavery and driving many black Americans to flee to Canada."}, {"q": "What was popular sovereignty as Stephen Douglas defined it?", "a": "The doctrine that settlers in each territory, not Congress, should vote on whether to allow slavery."}, {"q": "How did the Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 reshape party politics?", "a": "By repealing the Missouri Compromise, it enraged the North, destroyed the Whig Party, and gave rise to the sectional Republican Party."}, {"q": "What was free labor ideology?", "a": "The Republican belief that northern society let wage earners rise to independence, while slavery degraded labor and had to be kept out of the West."}, {"q": "Who were the Know-Nothings?", "a": "A nativist, anti-Catholic party that surged in 1854 amid Irish and German immigration before the slavery issue eclipsed nativism."}, {"q": "What was 'Bleeding Kansas'?", "a": "The mid-1850s guerrilla war between proslavery and free-state settlers that killed about 200 people and discredited popular sovereignty."}, {"q": "What happened in the caning of Charles Sumner?", "a": "In 1856 Representative Preston Brooks beat Senator Sumner with a cane on the Senate floor, symbolizing the breakdown of civil debate."}, {"q": "What did the Dred Scott decision (1857) rule?", "a": "Chief Justice Taney ruled black people could not be citizens and Congress could not bar slavery from territories, voiding the Missouri Compromise."}, {"q": "Why did the Dred Scott ruling make compromise harder?", "a": "It eliminated middle-ground solutions by barring Congress from restricting slavery anywhere, undercutting both Republicans and popular sovereignty."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the Lincoln-Douglas debates of 1858?", "a": "Though Douglas kept his Senate seat, Lincoln gained national fame by framing slavery as a moral wrong that must not spread."}, {"q": "What did John Brown's 1859 raid on Harpers Ferry do?", "a": "His attempt to spark a slave uprising failed and he was executed, but he became a northern martyr while terrifying the South."}, {"q": "Why was the election of 1860 essentially two sectional contests?", "a": "The party field split by region, and Lincoln won without any southern support, convincing the Deep South secession was its only protection."}, {"q": "Which state seceded first and when?", "a": "South Carolina seceded in December 1860, soon followed by six more Deep South states forming the Confederacy."}, {"q": "What began the Civil War in April 1861?", "a": "Confederate forces bombarded Fort Sumter in Charleston harbor after Lincoln tried to resupply it, opening the war."}, {"q": "Why did repeated compromise efforts like the Crittenden plan fail?", "a": "The North's commitment to stopping slavery's expansion and the South's insistence on protecting it had become irreconcilable."}, {"q": "How did Commodore Perry extend U.S. expansionism abroad in 1854?", "a": "He forced open Japanese ports to American trade, showing manifest destiny operating beyond the continent."}, {"q": "Compare the Whig and Republican parties on slavery's expansion.", "a": "The Whigs straddled the issue and collapsed after 1854; the Republicans arose as an explicitly sectional, free-labor party opposing slavery's spread."}, {"q": "What was the 'slave power' in Republican rhetoric?", "a": "The perceived conspiracy of slaveholding elites seeking to expand slavery and dominate national politics, threatening free labor's opportunity."}, {"q": "How did the Fugitive Slave Act energize the Underground Railroad?", "a": "By threatening free and escaped black people, it spurred abolitionists and communities to help fugitives reach safety in Canada."}, {"q": "Why did the Mexican War, not abolitionism, inject slavery into national politics?", "a": "Its vast territorial gains forced Congress to decide whether new lands would be slave or free, making expansion the central issue."}, {"q": "What role did railroad and territorial ambitions play in the Kansas-Nebraska Act?", "a": "Douglas wanted to organize the territories to secure a northern transcontinental railroad route, offering popular sovereignty to win southern votes."}], "14": [{"q": "Why is the Civil War called the first modern war?", "a": "Mass armies used industrial weapons like the rifled musket, while railroads, telegraphs, ironclads, and photography transformed warfare, killing about 750,000."}, {"q": "What advantages did the Union hold over the Confederacy?", "a": "Far greater population, industry, and financial resources, though it faced the harder task of conquering a vast, motivated South."}, {"q": "What happened at the First Battle of Bull Run (July 1861)?", "a": "Confederate forces routed Union troops near Manassas, shattering northern hopes for a quick and easy victory."}, {"q": "Why was General McClellan a frustration for Lincoln?", "a": "His excessive caution produced stalemate in the East, letting the Confederacy hold on despite Union strength."}, {"q": "Where did Grant win early Union victories in the West?", "a": "At Forts Henry and Donelson and at Shiloh, and the navy captured New Orleans, giving the North momentum in the West."}, {"q": "Who were 'contrabands'?", "a": "Enslaved people who fled to Union lines and were treated as confiscated enemy property, their flight pushing the government toward emancipation."}, {"q": "Why did the Battle of Antietam (Sept. 1862) matter beyond the battlefield?", "a": "Halting Lee's invasion on the bloodiest single day in U.S. history gave Lincoln the victory he needed to announce emancipation."}, {"q": "What did the Emancipation Proclamation do, effective Jan. 1, 1863?", "a": "It freed slaves in Confederate-held territory and authorized black enlistment, redefining the war as a fight against slavery."}, {"q": "Why did Lincoln move from caution to emancipation?", "a": "Combined pressure from escaping slaves, abolitionists, and Radical Republicans, plus military necessity, shifted his calculation."}, {"q": "Who were the Radical Republicans?", "a": "The Republican faction pushing hardest and earliest for emancipation, black enlistment, and equal civil and political rights for African Americans."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the 54th Massachusetts?", "a": "This black regiment's costly 1863 assault on Fort Wagner helped prove black soldiers' courage; about 200,000 black men served the Union."}, {"q": "What did the Homestead Act of 1862 provide?", "a": "160 acres of public land to settlers who lived on and improved it for five years, part of filling the West with free labor."}, {"q": "How did the transcontinental railroad reflect wartime nation-building?", "a": "Chartered in 1862 and finished in 1869, it linked the coasts and expanded markets, while doming the Plains Indians' way of life."}, {"q": "What were the New York City draft riots of July 1863?", "a": "Four days of violence in which mostly Irish immigrant mobs attacked draft offices and black New Yorkers, killing over 100 people."}, {"q": "Why did King Cotton diplomacy fail?", "a": "Withholding cotton did not force British intervention; Britain instead developed new sources in Egypt, India, and Central Asia."}, {"q": "What made the Battle of Gettysburg (July 1863) a turning point?", "a": "The largest battle in North America repulsed Lee's second northern invasion, climaxing with the failure of Pickett's Charge."}, {"q": "Why was the fall of Vicksburg (July 4, 1863) important?", "a": "Grant's siege gave the Union control of the Mississippi River and split the Confederacy in two."}, {"q": "What was Sherman's March to the Sea?", "a": "His late-1864 campaign from Atlanta to Savannah destroyed railroads, supplies, and southern morale, waging modern war on the enemy's society."}, {"q": "How did Grant's strategy in 1864 differ from earlier Union generals?", "a": "He waged a relentless war of attrition in Virginia, accepting heavy casualties to wear down Lee's smaller army."}, {"q": "Why did Sherman's capture of Atlanta help Lincoln politically?", "a": "The victory boosted northern morale and helped secure Lincoln's reelection in 1864."}, {"q": "What internal problems crippled the Confederacy?", "a": "Runaway inflation, shortages, desertion, and class resentment over policies like the 'twenty-negro' draft exemption eroded morale."}, {"q": "What was the 'twenty-negro' exemption and why did it cause resentment?", "a": "It excused one white man from the draft for every twenty slaves owned, fueling poorer whites' complaint of a 'rich man's war, poor man's fight.'"}, {"q": "What was Lincoln's Ten-Percent Plan?", "a": "A lenient 1863 Reconstruction proposal letting a state rejoin once 10 percent of its 1860 voters swore loyalty; it gave blacks no political role."}, {"q": "How did the Wade-Davis Bill differ from Lincoln's plan?", "a": "The 1864 congressional plan demanded a loyalty majority and guaranteed black legal equality; Lincoln killed it with a pocket veto."}, {"q": "What did the Thirteenth Amendment accomplish?", "a": "Approved by Congress in January 1865 and ratified that year, it permanently abolished slavery throughout the United States."}, {"q": "Where and when did Lee surrender?", "a": "Lee surrendered to Grant at Appomattox Court House in Virginia on April 9, 1865, effectively ending the war."}, {"q": "Who assassinated Lincoln and when?", "a": "John Wilkes Booth shot Lincoln at Ford's Theatre on April 14, 1865, days after Lee's surrender."}, {"q": "Why is wartime legislation called a 'Second American Revolution'?", "a": "The Homestead and Morrill Acts, railroad charters, national banking, greenbacks, and taxes vastly expanded federal power and capitalist development."}, {"q": "How did the war affect Native peoples in the West?", "a": "Western campaigns devastated tribes like the Navajo and Sioux, including the Navajo Long Walk and the Sand Creek Massacre."}, {"q": "What were the wartime 'rehearsals for Reconstruction'?", "a": "Experiments with free labor and black rights in the Sea Islands and Louisiana previewed later struggles over land and equality."}], "15": [{"q": "How did freedpeople define freedom after the Civil War?", "a": "Expansively—reuniting families, building independent churches and schools, voting, and above all owning land."}, {"q": "What was Sherman's Special Field Order 15?", "a": "His January 1865 order reserving coastal Carolina and Georgia land for black families in 40-acre plots, inspiring 'forty acres and a mule.'"}, {"q": "Why did land reform fail during Reconstruction?", "a": "President Johnson returned confiscated land to ex-Confederates, killing redistribution and leaving most freedpeople economically dependent."}, {"q": "What was the Freedmen's Bureau?", "a": "The 1865 federal agency that aided former slaves and refugees with schools, health care, and labor contracts, making major gains in education."}, {"q": "What was sharecropping?", "a": "A labor system where families farmed a landowner's plot for a share of the crop; it ended gang labor but locked farmers into poverty."}, {"q": "How did the crop-lien system trap farmers in debt?", "a": "Merchants advanced supplies against future crops at high interest, so falling cotton prices left many black and white farmers permanently indebted."}, {"q": "What were the Black Codes?", "a": "Laws by Johnson-era southern governments (1865-66) granting freedpeople minimal rights while forcing them into plantation labor."}, {"q": "Why did the Black Codes anger the North?", "a": "They convinced Republicans that the South rejected the meaning of emancipation, prompting Congress to seize control of Reconstruction."}, {"q": "What made the Civil Rights Act of 1866 notable?", "a": "It was the first major law passed over a presidential veto, declaring all persons born in the U.S. citizens with equal legal rights."}, {"q": "What did the Fourteenth Amendment (1868) establish?", "a": "Birthright citizenship, privileges or immunities, and equal protection of the laws, becoming the foundation of modern civil rights law."}, {"q": "What did the Reconstruction Act of 1867 do?", "a": "It divided the South into five military districts and required new governments based on black male suffrage, launching Radical Reconstruction."}, {"q": "Why was Andrew Johnson impeached in 1868?", "a": "For violating the Tenure of Office Act by firing Secretary of War Stanton; the Senate fell one vote short of removing him."}, {"q": "What did the Fifteenth Amendment (1870) guarantee?", "a": "It barred denying the vote based on race, though it left loopholes like literacy tests and poll taxes and excluded women."}, {"q": "Why did the Fifteenth Amendment split the women's rights movement?", "a": "Its exclusion of women from voting guarantees fractured the long alliance between feminists and abolitionists."}, {"q": "Who were carpetbaggers and scalawags?", "a": "Hostile labels for Reconstruction's white Republicans: northern migrants to the South and southern-born white Unionists."}, {"q": "What was America's first experiment in interracial democracy?", "a": "Radical Reconstruction produced about 2,000 black officeholders, new state constitutions, public schools, and civil rights laws."}, {"q": "What was the Ku Klux Klan during Reconstruction?", "a": "A terrorist group founded in Tennessee in 1866 that acted as the Democratic Party's armed wing, attacking black and white Republicans."}, {"q": "How did the Enforcement Acts affect the Klan?", "a": "The 1870-71 laws outlawed terrorist societies and let Grant deploy the army; his prosecutions broke the Klan by 1872."}, {"q": "What was the Colfax Massacre of 1873?", "a": "The slaughter of hundreds of black Louisianans by armed whites; the Supreme Court later overturned the convictions in U.S. v. Cruikshank."}, {"q": "Who were the Redeemers?", "a": "Southern Democrats who claimed to rescue their states from Republican rule, regaining power through elections and violence in the 1870s."}, {"q": "What was the Bargain of 1877?", "a": "The settlement of the disputed Hayes-Tilden election giving Republicans the presidency in exchange for ending federal intervention in the South."}, {"q": "How did the Slaughterhouse Cases (1873) weaken the 14th Amendment?", "a": "The Supreme Court narrowly interpreted its protections, limiting the federal government's power to safeguard citizens' rights."}, {"q": "What factors caused Reconstruction to collapse?", "a": "Klan terrorism, northern fatigue, the 1873 depression, hostile Court rulings, and the Bargain of 1877 together doomed it."}, {"q": "Who was Hiram Revels?", "a": "In 1870 he became the first black U.S. senator, representing Mississippi during Radical Reconstruction."}, {"q": "How did Presidential Reconstruction under Johnson differ from Congress's approach?", "a": "Johnson's lenient plan let ex-Confederates regain power, while Congress imposed military rule, black suffrage, and stronger federal protections."}, {"q": "Why did the Liberal Republican revolt of 1872 matter?", "a": "Reformers bolted the party and backed Horace Greeley, signaling declining northern commitment to enforcing Reconstruction."}, {"q": "What role did black churches and schools play after the war?", "a": "They became the enduring institutional foundation of African American community life and independence."}, {"q": "How did the Reconstruction amendments shift federal power?", "a": "They made the federal government the guardian of individual rights, shifting authority away from the states."}, {"q": "Why is Reconstruction called an 'unfinished revolution' despite its failure?", "a": "Its amendments—13th, 14th, and 15th—later became the constitutional basis for the 20th-century civil rights movement."}, {"q": "What was the Mississippi Plan?", "a": "The 1875 campaign of violence and intimidation Democrats used to overthrow Republican rule and end Reconstruction in Mississippi."}], "16": [{"q": "What was the second industrial revolution?", "a": "The post-Civil War surge in factories, railroads, steel, and electricity that made the U.S. the world's largest industrial economy."}, {"q": "How did the federal government aid rapid industrialization?", "a": "Through tariffs, railroad land grants, and military removal of Indians, contradicting the era's laissez-faire reputation."}, {"q": "Why were railroads central to Gilded Age growth?", "a": "They tripled in mileage, created a national market, enabled mass production, and even standardized national time zones in 1883."}, {"q": "What was vertical integration, and who perfected it?", "a": "Controlling every stage of production from raw materials to distribution; Andrew Carnegie perfected it in his steel empire."}, {"q": "What was horizontal expansion, and who exemplified it?", "a": "Growth by absorbing competitors in the same business; Rockefeller merged rival refineries into Standard Oil, controlling 90% of refining."}, {"q": "What did the term 'robber barons' express?", "a": "A hostile view of industrialists like Carnegie and Rockefeller as men who crushed competitors and unions while wielding unaccountable power."}, {"q": "How unequal was Gilded Age wealth by 1890?", "a": "The richest 1 percent held as much income as the entire bottom half of the population."}, {"q": "Who documented urban poverty, and how?", "a": "Jacob Riis exposed tenement poverty in 'How the Other Half Lives' (1890), using photographs to reveal how the poor lived."}, {"q": "What were bonanza farms?", "a": "Huge western farms of thousands of acres worked by wage laborers, foreshadowing corporate, mechanized agribusiness."}, {"q": "What happened at the Battle of the Little Bighorn (1876)?", "a": "Sioux and Cheyenne under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse wiped out Custer's cavalry while defending the Black Hills."}, {"q": "What did the Dawes Act of 1887 do?", "a": "It broke tribal lands into individual allotments and sold the 'surplus' to whites, aiming at assimilation; Indians lost about 86 million acres."}, {"q": "What was the Wounded Knee massacre (1890)?", "a": "The army's killing of 150-200 Sioux, many Ghost Dance followers, in South Dakota, ending armed Native resistance."}, {"q": "Why did the destruction of the buffalo devastate Plains Indians?", "a": "The buffalo's extermination destroyed the economic and cultural foundation of Plains life, forcing tribes onto reservations."}, {"q": "Where does the name 'Gilded Age' come from?", "a": "From Twain and Warner's 1873 novel; it suggested glittering wealth masking corruption, inequality, and social conflict."}, {"q": "What prompted the Civil Service Act of 1883?", "a": "President Garfield's assassination by a disappointed office-seeker spurred merit-based exams to replace the patronage spoils system."}, {"q": "What was the Interstate Commerce Commission?", "a": "The first federal regulatory agency, created in 1887 to ensure fair railroad rates, though its lack of rate-setting power left it weak."}, {"q": "Why was the Sherman Antitrust Act (1890) initially ineffective?", "a": "Its vague wording made it hard to use against monopolies, and courts applied it mainly against striking labor unions."}, {"q": "What was Social Darwinism?", "a": "Applying 'survival of the fittest' to society to argue inequality was natural and that aid to the poor or business regulation defied evolution."}, {"q": "Who was a leading American Social Darwinist?", "a": "William Graham Sumner argued that government aid to the poor and business regulation violated natural law."}, {"q": "What did the doctrine of 'liberty of contract' hold?", "a": "That workers and employers must bargain freely without state interference; courts used it to strike down labor protections."}, {"q": "What did Lochner v. New York (1905) decide?", "a": "The Supreme Court voided a maximum-hours law for bakers, enshrining liberty of contract over worker protections."}, {"q": "What was the Knights of Labor?", "a": "The first mass national labor union, led by Terence Powderly, that organized skilled and unskilled, women, and black workers, peaking near 800,000."}, {"q": "What was the Haymarket Affair (1886)?", "a": "A bombing at a Chicago labor protest killed a policeman; eight anarchists were convicted on flimsy evidence, discrediting the labor movement."}, {"q": "What was the Great Railroad Strike of 1877?", "a": "The first national labor uprising, sparked by wage cuts, which federal troops suppressed after widespread violence."}, {"q": "What alternative to unregulated capitalism did Henry George propose?", "a": "In 'Progress and Poverty' (1879) he proposed a 'single tax' on land to combat inequality without hindering enterprise."}, {"q": "What was Edward Bellamy's contribution to the freedom debate?", "a": "His 1888 bestseller 'Looking Backward' imagined a cooperative, egalitarian utopia as an alternative to Gilded Age competition."}, {"q": "What was the Social Gospel?", "a": "A movement urging Christians to apply a religious ethic of solidarity and justice to industrial society's poverty and inequality."}, {"q": "Compare 'captains of industry' with 'robber barons.'", "a": "Both terms described the same men; 'captains of industry' praised their innovation, while 'robber barons' condemned their ruthlessness."}, {"q": "What was the Crédit Mobilier scandal?", "a": "An 1872 fraud tied to Union Pacific railroad construction that bribed officials, epitomizing Gilded Age political corruption."}, {"q": "How did courts and reformers disagree over freedom's meaning?", "a": "Courts and Social Darwinists defined freedom as an unregulated market, while labor and reformers argued freedom required economic equality."}], "17": [{"q": "What was the People's (Populist) Party and when did it form?", "a": "A party formed in 1892 out of the Farmers' Alliance to represent indebted farmers and some workers, demanding federal action against corporate power."}, {"q": "What four key reforms did the 1892 Omaha platform demand?", "a": "A graduated income tax, direct election of senators, government control of currency, and public ownership of railroads."}, {"q": "Why did the Homestead Strike of 1892 end in defeat for workers?", "a": "Pinkerton guards and the state militia reopened Carnegie's steel plant, destroying the union and proving corporations could crush even strong labor."}, {"q": "Who led the Pullman Strike and how was it broken?", "a": "Eugene V. Debs and the American Railway Union led the 1894 boycott; a federal court injunction and troops broke it, and In re Debs upheld his jailing."}, {"q": "What was Coxey's Army?", "a": "A 1894 march of unemployed men led by Jacob Coxey to Washington demanding federal jobs and currency expansion during the depression; soldiers dispersed it."}, {"q": "Why did the 1893 depression matter politically?", "a": "It sparked Coxey's Army and the Pullman Strike, deepened farmer and worker unrest, and set the stage for the pivotal 1896 election."}, {"q": "What was Bryan's 'Cross of Gold' speech about?", "a": "It attacked the gold standard and championed free silver, winning William Jennings Bryan the 1896 Democratic nomination."}, {"q": "Compare the 1896 coalitions of Bryan and McKinley.", "a": "Bryan drew the agrarian South and West on free silver; McKinley drew the industrial Northeast and Midwest, building a lasting Republican majority."}, {"q": "Why was the election of 1896 a 'realigning' election?", "a": "McKinley's well-funded win forged a Republican majority that dominated national politics until 1932."}, {"q": "How did southern states disenfranchise Black voters after 1890?", "a": "Through poll taxes, literacy tests, understanding clauses, and grandfather clauses that evaded the Fifteenth Amendment on paper."}, {"q": "What was a grandfather clause and whom did it protect?", "a": "A rule exempting men whose ancestors voted before the Civil War from new voting requirements, shielding illiterate whites while excluding Black voters."}, {"q": "What did Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) establish?", "a": "That state-mandated racial segregation was constitutional if facilities were 'separate but equal,' giving legal cover to Jim Crow."}, {"q": "Who dissented in Plessy and what did he argue?", "a": "Justice John Marshall Harlan, who argued the Constitution is 'color-blind' and tolerates no class among citizens."}, {"q": "How was the Jim Crow system enforced through terror?", "a": "By lynching—mob murders of Black men used to intimidate African Americans and uphold white supremacy."}, {"q": "Who led the national anti-lynching crusade?", "a": "Journalist Ida B. Wells, who documented lynchings and campaigned against mob violence."}, {"q": "What did Booker T. Washington urge in the Atlanta Compromise (1895)?", "a": "That Black southerners accept segregation for the time being and focus on vocational training and economic self-help."}, {"q": "How did W. E. B. Du Bois differ from Booker T. Washington?", "a": "Du Bois rejected accommodation and demanded full civil and political rights rather than Washington's economic patience."}, {"q": "What was the Kansas Exodus?", "a": "The 1879–1880 migration of tens of thousands of Black southerners to Kansas seeking land, schooling, and safety from post-Reconstruction violence."}, {"q": "Why was the Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) historically significant?", "a": "It was the first federal law to bar an immigrant group on racial grounds, and was renewed and made permanent in 1902."}, {"q": "How did the AFL under Gompers limit its membership?", "a": "It practiced 'business unionism,' organizing mainly skilled, white, native-born men and ignoring the unskilled and immigrants."}, {"q": "What event helped trigger the Spanish-American War in 1898?", "a": "The explosion of the U.S.S. Maine in Havana harbor, amplified by the yellow press, though the blast was likely accidental."}, {"q": "What territories did the U.S. gain from the Spanish-American War?", "a": "Puerto Rico, Guam, and the Philippines, while Cuba became a protectorate and Hawaii was annexed in 1898."}, {"q": "How did the U.S. acquire Hawaii?", "a": "American planters overthrew Queen Liliuokalani in 1893, and the U.S. annexed the islands in 1898 during the Spanish-American War."}, {"q": "What did the Platt Amendment (1901) do to Cuba?", "a": "It authorized U.S. military intervention and naval bases like Guantánamo, making Cuba an American protectorate."}, {"q": "Why was the Philippine War (1899–1903) significant?", "a": "It required a bloody campaign against Emilio Aguinaldo's independence movement, exposing the violence behind 'benevolent' imperialism."}, {"q": "What did the Insular Cases decide?", "a": "That constitutional rights did not fully extend to new territories, allowing the U.S. to govern colonial subjects without their consent."}, {"q": "What was the Open Door Policy?", "a": "John Hay's 1899 declaration seeking equal trading access for all nations in China, protecting American commercial interests."}, {"q": "Why did farmers favor free silver and currency inflation?", "a": "Inflation would raise crop prices and ease repayment of debts, countering the deflation caused by the gold standard."}, {"q": "How did the 1900 election frame the debate over empire?", "a": "McKinley's rematch victory over Bryan was cast as a choice of 'republic or empire' amid debate over overseas colonies."}, {"q": "How did the boundaries of American freedom narrow in the 1890s?", "a": "Through Jim Crow, Black disenfranchisement, Chinese exclusion, nativism, a narrower AFL, and suffragists' embrace of racial hierarchy."}], "18": [{"q": "Who were the muckrakers?", "a": "Investigative journalists and novelists like Lincoln Steffens and Upton Sinclair whose exposés of corruption and abuse built public support for reform."}, {"q": "What was 'Fordism'?", "a": "Henry Ford's economic model combining assembly-line mass production with wages high enough for workers to buy the goods they made."}, {"q": "Why did Ford introduce the five-dollar day?", "a": "To reduce turnover on the assembly line and enable workers to afford the mass-produced goods driving the consumer economy."}, {"q": "What did the 1911 Triangle Shirtwaist fire reveal and cause?", "a": "It killed about 146 mostly young immigrant women trapped behind locked doors, galvanizing labor and spurring new factory-safety laws."}, {"q": "What was scientific management?", "a": "Frederick W. Taylor's system using time-and-motion studies to dictate the 'one best way' to work, boosting efficiency but stripping workers of control."}, {"q": "How did Progressives view government's role?", "a": "As a tool to be used at city, state, and federal levels to humanize industrial capitalism and address urban-industrial problems."}, {"q": "What was the Socialist Party's high point?", "a": "The 1912 election, when Eugene V. Debs won about 6 percent of the presidential vote on a platform of public ownership of industry."}, {"q": "How did the IWW differ from the AFL?", "a": "Founded in 1905, the IWW welcomed unskilled workers, immigrants, women, and Black workers whom the craft-focused AFL excluded."}, {"q": "What did Margaret Sanger fight for?", "a": "The birth-control movement, arguing women could not be free without controlling reproduction; she was jailed for opening a Brooklyn clinic in 1916."}, {"q": "What was Hull House and who founded it?", "a": "A Chicago settlement house founded by Jane Addams in 1889 where reformers lived among the immigrant poor and lobbied for social legislation."}, {"q": "What did Muller v. Oregon (1908) decide?", "a": "It upheld maximum working hours for women, using Louis Brandeis's data-filled brief but casting women as dependents needing protection."}, {"q": "What was the Wisconsin Idea?", "a": "Governor Robert La Follette's program of direct primaries, corporate taxes, railroad regulation, and use of university experts as a model for reform."}, {"q": "What did the Seventeenth Amendment (1913) change?", "a": "It required the direct popular election of U.S. senators instead of selection by state legislatures."}, {"q": "What was Theodore Roosevelt's Square Deal?", "a": "His domestic agenda of policing 'bad' trusts, regulating railroads, and protecting consumers via cases like Northern Securities and laws like the Hepburn Act."}, {"q": "What sparked the Meat Inspection and Pure Food and Drug Acts of 1906?", "a": "Upton Sinclair's novel The Jungle, which exposed filthy conditions in the meatpacking industry."}, {"q": "How did John Muir and Gifford Pinchot differ on conservation?", "a": "Pinchot favored scientific management and use of natural resources; Muir wanted to preserve wilderness for its own sake."}, {"q": "Contrast New Nationalism and New Freedom in 1912.", "a": "Roosevelt's New Nationalism accepted big business but demanded strong federal regulation; Wilson's New Freedom aimed to restore competition by breaking up monopolies."}, {"q": "Why was the 1912 election a debate over the state's role?", "a": "It pitted competing visions—Wilson's New Freedom versus Roosevelt's New Nationalism—over whether to regulate or dismantle concentrated economic power."}, {"q": "Who ran in the four-way election of 1912 and who won?", "a": "Woodrow Wilson (Democrat) won over Theodore Roosevelt (Progressive), William Howard Taft (Republican), and Eugene Debs (Socialist)."}, {"q": "What major agencies did Wilson create?", "a": "The Federal Reserve (1913) to control banking and currency, and the Federal Trade Commission (1914) to police unfair business practices."}, {"q": "Why did Taft split the Republican Party?", "a": "His actions alienated Roosevelt's wing, prompting Roosevelt to run as a third-party Progressive in 1912 and divide Republican voters."}, {"q": "What were the antidemocratic sides of Progressivism?", "a": "Faith in unelected experts, literacy tests, voting restrictions, and tolerance of southern Black disenfranchisement and Jim Crow."}, {"q": "How did immigration change American cities by 1920?", "a": "About 13 million immigrants arrived from 1901–1914, and for the first time more Americans lived in cities than on farms."}, {"q": "Where did most Progressive-era immigrants enter the U.S.?", "a": "Through Ellis Island in the East and Angel Island in the West, plus growing Mexican migration into the Southwest."}, {"q": "What did the Sixteenth Amendment authorize?", "a": "It permitted a federal income tax, ratified in 1913 and backed by Taft, giving the government a new revenue source."}, {"q": "What was the Northern Securities case?", "a": "A 1904 Supreme Court ruling ordering a giant railroad holding company dissolved, a landmark antitrust victory for Roosevelt."}, {"q": "What did the Clayton Act (1914) do for unions?", "a": "It shielded labor unions from antitrust prosecution, addressing a key labor grievance."}, {"q": "How did women drive Progressive reform?", "a": "Through settlement houses, maternalist legislation, the birth-control movement, and a revived mass campaign for woman suffrage."}, {"q": "What labor reforms capped Wilson's first term in 1916?", "a": "The Keating-Owen child-labor law and the Adamson Act establishing an eight-hour day for railroad workers."}, {"q": "How did mass consumption reshape ideas of freedom?", "a": "Freedom became tied to consumer abundance, with the Model T and rising living standards symbolizing the new American good life."}], "19": [{"q": "What was Wilson's liberal internationalism?", "a": "The belief that spreading American trade, investment, and values abroad would advance both U.S. interests and global political freedom."}, {"q": "What did the Roosevelt Corollary (1904) claim?", "a": "It extended the Monroe Doctrine, asserting a U.S. right to act as an 'international police power' in the Western Hemisphere."}, {"q": "How did the U.S. gain the Panama Canal Zone?", "a": "Roosevelt backed Panama's 1903 secession from Colombia, and the U.S. gained sovereignty over the Canal Zone; the canal opened in 1914."}, {"q": "What was Dollar Diplomacy?", "a": "Taft's policy of using American financial and business investment, rather than direct force, to extend U.S. influence abroad."}, {"q": "Why is Wilson's foreign policy called 'moral imperialism'?", "a": "He intervened in Latin America more than any predecessor—occupying Haiti and the Dominican Republic and chasing Pancho Villa—claiming to promote freedom."}, {"q": "How did the sinking of the Lusitania (1915) affect the U.S.?", "a": "A German U-boat killed 124 Americans aboard the British liner, outraging opinion and fueling Wilson's preparedness program."}, {"q": "What was the Zimmermann Telegram?", "a": "An intercepted 1917 German message urging Mexico to join a war against the U.S. for lost territory; its release helped push America into the war."}, {"q": "What immediate cause drove the U.S. into WWI in April 1917?", "a": "Germany's resumption of unrestricted submarine warfare, combined with the Zimmermann Telegram."}, {"q": "What did Wilson mean by making the world 'safe for democracy'?", "a": "He framed U.S. entry into WWI as an idealistic crusade to spread democratic self-government, not mere self-interest."}, {"q": "What were the Fourteen Points (1918)?", "a": "Wilson's war aims, including self-determination, freedom of the seas, open diplomacy, free trade, and a League of Nations."}, {"q": "What did the Selective Service Act (1917) accomplish?", "a": "It required 24 million men to register for the draft, rapidly building the army that fought in France."}, {"q": "What was the War Industries Board?", "a": "A federal agency under Bernard Baruch that directed wartime production, allocated raw materials, and set prices."}, {"q": "What did the Committee on Public Information do?", "a": "Led by George Creel, it flooded the country with pro-war pamphlets, posters, and films selling the war as a fight for freedom."}, {"q": "What did the Espionage and Sedition Acts criminalize?", "a": "Interference with the draft and speech critical of the government or war effort; over 2,000 people, including Debs, were prosecuted."}, {"q": "Why are the WWI years called the harshest assault on civil liberties?", "a": "The Espionage and Sedition Acts, the jailing of dissenters like Debs, and coercive '100 percent Americanism' crushed free speech."}, {"q": "How did war fervor help pass Prohibition?", "a": "Wartime calls for sacrifice and hostility to German-American brewers boosted support for the Eighteenth Amendment, ratified in 1919."}, {"q": "How did women win the Nineteenth Amendment?", "a": "Through wartime service and the militant tactics of Alice Paul's National Woman's Party, securing the vote nationwide in 1920."}, {"q": "What was the Great Migration?", "a": "The movement of about half a million African Americans from the South to northern cities for jobs, schooling, and escape from lynching."}, {"q": "What violent backlash met the Great Migration?", "a": "Race riots in cities like East St. Louis, Chicago, and Tulsa as white residents attacked Black newcomers."}, {"q": "What was the NAACP and when was it founded?", "a": "A civil rights organization founded in 1909 by Du Bois and white reformers that fought discrimination through lawsuits and its journal, The Crisis."}, {"q": "What did Marcus Garvey advocate?", "a": "Black pride, self-reliance, and African independence through the Universal Negro Improvement Association, the era's largest Black mass movement."}, {"q": "What was the Red Scare of 1919–1920?", "a": "A postwar panic over radicalism in which Palmer's raids arrested thousands without warrants and deported immigrant radicals."}, {"q": "Why did the 1919 strike wave fail?", "a": "It coincided with the Red Scare, which equated labor militancy with radicalism and helped employers and government crush the strikes."}, {"q": "Why did the U.S. Senate reject the Treaty of Versailles?", "a": "Senators feared the League of Nations would drag the U.S. into future wars and erode congressional war powers."}, {"q": "How did the Versailles Treaty disappoint colonized peoples?", "a": "Despite the Fourteen Points' promise of self-determination, the treaty ignored colonial independence and preserved European empires."}, {"q": "Where did American troops help turn the tide of WWI?", "a": "At the Meuse-Argonne offensive in 1918, contributing to the Allied push that led to the November armistice."}, {"q": "What was Harding's 'return to normalcy'?", "a": "His 1920 campaign promise to abandon Wilsonian idealism and wartime turmoil for stability, winning him the presidency."}, {"q": "What happened in the 1921 Tulsa riot?", "a": "White mobs destroyed a prosperous Black community, in what became one of the deadliest episodes of racial violence in U.S. history."}, {"q": "How did WWI expand the federal government?", "a": "Through the draft, the War Industries Board, high taxes and Liberty bonds, and propaganda, creating a powerful but temporary national state."}, {"q": "How did the war affect labor before 1919?", "a": "Wartime labor shortages briefly empowered unions and raised wages, gains reversed by the defeat of the 1919 strike wave."}], "20": [{"q": "What industries drove 1920s economic growth?", "a": "Autos, chemicals, radio, and household appliances, with American multinationals dominating world markets."}, {"q": "How did consumer culture redefine freedom in the 1920s?", "a": "It equated freedom with private consumption—installment buying, movies, and celebrity heroes—replacing thrift with credit-fueled spending."}, {"q": "Who were the flappers?", "a": "Young urban women who flouted prewar conventions with bobbed hair, short skirts, smoking, and open sexuality, embodying new personal freedom."}, {"q": "Why did the Equal Rights Amendment split the feminist movement?", "a": "Alice Paul's 1923 ERA would erase all sex-based legal distinctions, but most women's groups feared it would void protective labor laws for women."}, {"q": "Who benefited least from 1920s prosperity?", "a": "Farmers sank into depression, unions lost over 2 million members, and about 40 percent of Americans remained poor."}, {"q": "What was the Teapot Dome scandal?", "a": "A Harding-era scandal in which Interior Secretary Albert Fall took bribes to lease federal oil reserves, becoming the first cabinet officer convicted of a felony."}, {"q": "What was the 'American Plan' / welfare capitalism?", "a": "An employer strategy pairing the union-free open shop with company pensions and benefits, which shrank organized labor in the 1920s."}, {"q": "What did Adkins v. Children's Hospital (1923) decide?", "a": "It struck down a women's minimum-wage law, reasoning that enfranchised women no longer needed special workplace protection, retreating from Muller."}, {"q": "How did Harding and Coolidge serve business?", "a": "Through tax cuts, high tariffs, anti-union policy, and a conservative Supreme Court that rolled back Progressive regulation."}, {"q": "What did the Sacco-Vanzetti case reveal?", "a": "Deep nativist prejudice, as two Italian immigrant anarchists were convicted on thin evidence and executed in 1927 amid anti-radical hysteria."}, {"q": "How was the second Ku Klux Klan different from the first?", "a": "Revived in 1915, it was a mass movement of millions strong in the North and West, targeting immigrants, Catholics, and Jews as well as Blacks."}, {"q": "What did the Immigration Act of 1924 do?", "a": "It set national-origins quotas slashing southern and eastern European immigration, barred Asians entirely, and created the Border Patrol and 'illegal alien.'"}, {"q": "Why is the Immigration Act of 1924 significant?", "a": "It closed the 'golden door' to Europe and Asia and invented the legal category of the 'illegal alien,' reshaping American immigration for decades."}, {"q": "What was the Scopes trial (1925) about?", "a": "The Tennessee prosecution of teacher John Scopes for teaching evolution, becoming a national showdown between fundamentalism and modern secularism."}, {"q": "Who faced off at the Scopes trial?", "a": "Clarence Darrow defended Scopes and famously grilled William Jennings Bryan, who championed the prosecution and biblical literalism."}, {"q": "What was fundamentalism?", "a": "A Protestant movement insisting on the literal truth of the Bible and battling modernism, Darwinism, and loosened moral codes."}, {"q": "When and why was the ACLU founded?", "a": "The American Civil Liberties Union formed in 1920 out of wartime free-speech defense and litigated landmark cases protecting expression."}, {"q": "What test came out of Schenck v. United States (1919)?", "a": "Holmes's 'clear and present danger' test, which upheld the Espionage Act; later Holmes-Brandeis dissents seeded stronger speech protection."}, {"q": "What did Meyer v. Nebraska establish?", "a": "It gave cultural pluralism constitutional footing by protecting the teaching of foreign languages, striking down a wartime ban."}, {"q": "What was the Harlem Renaissance?", "a": "A 1920s flowering of Black literature, music, and art in New York—Hughes, McKay, Hurston—expressing the defiant spirit of the 'New Negro.'"}, {"q": "Who was the first Catholic major-party presidential nominee?", "a": "Al Smith, who lost to Herbert Hoover in the 1928 landslide amid anti-Catholic prejudice."}, {"q": "What happened on Black Tuesday, October 29, 1929?", "a": "The stock market crashed, wiping out over $10 billion in value, shattering confidence, and helping trigger the Great Depression."}, {"q": "What structural weaknesses did the 1929 crash expose?", "a": "Unequal incomes, farm distress, rampant speculation, and a fragile international financial system."}, {"q": "How severe was the Depression by 1932?", "a": "Output had fallen by about a third and roughly a quarter of workers were unemployed."}, {"q": "What was the Smoot-Hawley Tariff (1930) and its effect?", "a": "A record-high tariff signed by Hoover that provoked foreign retaliation and shrank world trade, deepening the Depression."}, {"q": "What was the Reconstruction Finance Corporation (1932)?", "a": "Hoover's agency lending federal money to failing banks and businesses—a break with laissez-faire, though he refused direct relief to the unemployed."}, {"q": "Why did Hoover's response to the Depression fail?", "a": "His faith in voluntarist 'associational action' and limited direct relief proved inadequate to the scale of the economic catastrophe."}, {"q": "What happened to the Bonus marchers in 1932?", "a": "Unemployed WWI veterans demanding early bonus payment were driven from Washington by the army, damaging Hoover's reputation."}, {"q": "How did the Great Migration continue to shape the 1920s?", "a": "It kept moving Black southerners north, making Harlem the cultural capital of Black America even as Mexican migration grew."}, {"q": "How did the Depression discredit the business creed?", "a": "The collapse under pro-business Republican rule undermined faith in laissez-faire and prepared the way for a new liberalism."}], "21": [{"q": "Who defeated Herbert Hoover in the 1932 election and what did he promise?", "a": "Franklin D. Roosevelt won in a landslide amid the Great Depression, promising Americans a 'new deal.'"}, {"q": "What was the 'Hundred Days'?", "a": "The productive opening months of FDR's presidency in spring 1933, when Congress passed roughly fifteen major laws creating the First New Deal's core agencies."}, {"q": "What did FDR do first to rescue the banking system in 1933?", "a": "He declared a national bank holiday, then reopened sound banks and created federal deposit insurance to restore public confidence."}, {"q": "How did the AAA try to raise farm prices, and who did it hurt?", "a": "It paid farmers to produce less, helping landowners but displacing tenants and sharecroppers from the land."}, {"q": "Why was the NRA controversial and what ended it?", "a": "Its industry codes were dominated by big firms; the Supreme Court declared it unconstitutional in the 1935 Schechter case."}, {"q": "What was the purpose of the TVA?", "a": "It built dams on the Tennessee River for flood control and sold cheap public electricity, competing directly with private utilities."}, {"q": "Contrast the goals of the First and Second New Deals.", "a": "The First New Deal (1933) focused on relief and recovery through experimental agencies; the Second (1935) built lasting security like Social Security and the Wagner Act."}, {"q": "What did the Wagner Act of 1935 guarantee workers?", "a": "It created a federal board to supervise union elections and banned unfair employer practices, giving labor real legal protection to organize."}, {"q": "What three programs did the Social Security Act of 1935 establish?", "a": "Old-age pensions, unemployment insurance, and aid to the disabled and dependent children—the foundation of the American welfare state."}, {"q": "Why did Social Security exclude agricultural and domestic workers?", "a": "The 'southern veto' demanded these exclusions to preserve cheap Black labor, cutting most African Americans out of coverage."}, {"q": "Who founded the CIO and what made it different from older unions?", "a": "John L. Lewis founded it in 1935 to organize all workers in mass-production industries by industry rather than by separate crafts."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the Flint sit-down strike?", "a": "The UAW occupied GM's Flint plants in 1936-1937 and won union recognition, a landmark victory for industrial unionism."}, {"q": "How did grassroots pressure push FDR further left?", "a": "The CIO's labor upheaval and demands from Huey Long, Townsend, and Coughlin for bolder redistribution helped spur the Second New Deal."}, {"q": "What was Huey Long's Share Our Wealth plan?", "a": "A 1934 proposal to confiscate great fortunes and guarantee every family an income; its popularity pressured FDR before Long's 1935 assassination."}, {"q": "What did the WPA accomplish?", "a": "It employed about three million people a year building roads, bridges, and airports and funded artists, writers, and theater."}, {"q": "What was FDR's court-packing plan and how did it turn out?", "a": "His 1937 bid to add justices to protect New Deal laws; Congress rejected it, but the Court soon stopped striking down economic regulation."}, {"q": "Why did the 1937 court-packing episode still produce a lasting shift?", "a": "The justices began upholding federal economic regulation, permanently accepting the New Deal's expansion of government power."}, {"q": "What caused the Dust Bowl?", "a": "Severe drought plus decades of over-plowing on the southern Plains let topsoil blow away in massive storms in the mid-1930s."}, {"q": "Where did many Dust Bowl families migrate?", "a": "More than a million farm families fled the Plains, many heading to California in search of work."}, {"q": "How did FHA policy deepen housing segregation?", "a": "The agency insured cheap mortgages that made homeownership affordable but refused loans in minority neighborhoods, entrenching redlining."}, {"q": "What was the New Deal coalition?", "a": "A durable Democratic alliance of workers, farmers, urban ethnics, northern Black voters, and the white South that dominated politics for decades."}, {"q": "Why did Black voters shift to the Democratic Party in the 1930s?", "a": "Despite unequal benefits, New Deal relief programs and FDR's leadership won their support, breaking the old loyalty to the party of Lincoln."}, {"q": "How did the New Deal redefine American liberalism?", "a": "It transformed liberalism from limited government into active federal responsibility for citizens' economic security."}, {"q": "What was the Indian New Deal?", "a": "John Collier's reforms, including the 1934 Indian Reorganization Act, which ended land allotment and forced assimilation and restored some tribal self-government."}, {"q": "What was the Scottsboro case?", "a": "The 1931 arrest of nine Black youths falsely accused of rape in Alabama, which became an international civil rights cause."}, {"q": "What did the CCC do?", "a": "It put millions of jobless young men to work on reforestation, flood control, and park projects for modest government wages."}, {"q": "What happened to the economy in 1937?", "a": "FDR's spending cuts triggered a sharp recession, pushing the administration toward Keynesian deficit spending to restart recovery."}, {"q": "What did the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 do?", "a": "It set a national minimum wage, established a maximum workweek, and banned child labor in interstate commerce."}, {"q": "Why is 1939 remembered for Marian Anderson?", "a": "Eleanor Roosevelt arranged her Lincoln Memorial concert after the DAR barred the Black singer from Constitution Hall, a powerful racial symbol."}, {"q": "What finally ended mass unemployment of the Depression?", "a": "Not the New Deal itself but World War II mobilization, which drove the massive production boom that put Americans back to work."}], "22": [{"q": "What were FDR's Four Freedoms?", "a": "Freedom of speech and worship, and freedom from want and fear—stated in January 1941 as the central language of American war purpose."}, {"q": "What did the Neutrality Acts of the 1930s do?", "a": "They banned arms sales and travel involving warring nations, reflecting the isolationist mood of Depression-era America."}, {"q": "Why did most Americans favor isolationism in the 1930s?", "a": "Memories of World War I's costs made them wary of foreign conflicts, even as Japan invaded China and Hitler expanded in Europe."}, {"q": "What was the Lend-Lease Act?", "a": "A 1941 law letting the U.S. supply arms to Britain, China, and later the USSR with repayment deferred, making America the 'arsenal of democracy.'"}, {"q": "What steps drew the U.S. toward the Allies before Pearl Harbor?", "a": "'Cash and carry' sales, Lend-Lease, and an oil embargo against Japan progressively abandoned strict neutrality."}, {"q": "What happened at Pearl Harbor?", "a": "Japan's surprise air attack on the U.S. Pacific fleet on December 7, 1941 killed over 2,000 servicemen and brought America into the war."}, {"q": "Why was the Battle of Midway a turning point?", "a": "In June 1942, U.S. forces aided by broken Japanese codes sank four enemy carriers, turning the tide of the Pacific war."}, {"q": "What was D-Day?", "a": "The June 6, 1944 Allied landing at Normandy under Eisenhower, the largest sea-land invasion in history, which opened the western front."}, {"q": "How did war mobilization affect the U.S. economy?", "a": "The gross national product more than doubled, unemployment vanished, and federal agencies directed production, prices, and labor."}, {"q": "What did 'Rosie the Riveter' symbolize?", "a": "The millions of women who took industrial jobs during the war, temporarily transforming women's role in the workforce."}, {"q": "What was the GI Bill of Rights?", "a": "The 1944 law giving veterans education benefits, unemployment pay, and low-cost mortgages, fueling postwar college enrollment and suburban growth."}, {"q": "How did Americans disagree over the meaning of 'freedom' during the war?", "a": "New Dealers pushed an Economic Bill of Rights while business promoted 'free enterprise,' aided by Hayek's Road to Serfdom."}, {"q": "What was Japanese-American internment?", "a": "The removal under Executive Order 9066 of over 110,000 people of Japanese descent—two-thirds citizens—from the West Coast to guarded camps."}, {"q": "What did the Supreme Court decide in Korematsu v. United States?", "a": "It upheld internment as constitutional in 1944; Justice Jackson warned the precedent lay ready 'like a loaded weapon' for future abuse."}, {"q": "What was the bracero program?", "a": "A 1942 U.S.-Mexico agreement bringing Mexican contract laborers to American farms; it lasted until 1964 and imported over 4.5 million workers."}, {"q": "What were the zoot suit riots?", "a": "1943 violence in Los Angeles where sailors and police attacked Mexican-American youths, exposing the gap between tolerance rhetoric and reality."}, {"q": "What was the Second Great Migration?", "a": "The wartime movement of roughly 700,000 Black southerners to northern and western industrial cities, reshaping urban America."}, {"q": "What was the Double-V campaign?", "a": "The Black press's demand for victory over fascism abroad and over segregation at home, seeding the modern civil rights movement."}, {"q": "How did A. Philip Randolph win the FEPC?", "a": "His threatened March on Washington pushed FDR to issue Executive Order 8802 in 1941, banning discrimination in defense industries."}, {"q": "How did the war against Nazism reshape ideas about race and ethnicity?", "a": "Fighting Nazi racism made pluralism official rhetoric and assimilated European ethnics as loyal Americans, discrediting racial hierarchy."}, {"q": "What was the Holocaust?", "a": "Nazi Germany's systematic murder of six million Jews and over a million other victims, the culmination of Hitler's racial ideology."}, {"q": "What decisions were made at the Bretton Woods conference?", "a": "The 1944 meeting made the dollar the world's key currency and created the World Bank and IMF, anchoring the postwar economic order."}, {"q": "How did the U.S. respond after Germany overran France in 1940?", "a": "FDR won a third term, launched the first peacetime draft, and increased aid to Britain to keep the Allies fighting."}, {"q": "What was the significance of Stalingrad?", "a": "The Soviet victory broke the German army on the eastern front, marking a decisive turning point in the war in Europe."}, {"q": "Who became president after FDR's death, and what major decision did he make?", "a": "Harry Truman succeeded FDR in April 1945 and ordered atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki to force Japan's surrender."}, {"q": "How did the atomic bombings end the war?", "a": "After Hiroshima and Nagasaki were destroyed in August 1945, Japan surrendered, ending World War II."}, {"q": "What was CORE and when was it founded?", "a": "The Congress of Racial Equality, founded in 1942, pioneered nonviolent direct action against segregation."}, {"q": "Compare Japanese-American internment with the treatment of German- and Italian-Americans.", "a": "Japanese-Americans were interned en masse by ancestry, while German- and Italian-Americans largely escaped such collective punishment, revealing racial bias."}, {"q": "How did the war affect the American West and South?", "a": "Massive defense spending built up West Coast and southern industry, pulling millions of workers into booming new production centers."}, {"q": "What new world order emerged from World War II?", "a": "The U.S. became the dominant power, shaping a system built on Bretton Woods, the United Nations, and rising Cold War tension with the USSR."}], "23": [{"q": "What was the Cold War?", "a": "The decades-long geopolitical and ideological struggle between the U.S. and the Soviet Union that began after World War II and shaped politics at home and abroad."}, {"q": "What did George Kennan's Long Telegram argue?", "a": "His 1946 dispatch from Moscow held that Soviet ideology made normal diplomacy impossible and that the U.S. must firmly resist Soviet expansion."}, {"q": "Define the policy of containment.", "a": "The strategy, derived from Kennan, of blocking any further expansion of Soviet power rather than rolling it back or accommodating it."}, {"q": "What did Churchill mean by the 'iron curtain'?", "a": "His 1946 phrase for the dividing line between the free West and Soviet-dominated eastern Europe."}, {"q": "What was the Truman Doctrine?", "a": "Truman's 1947 pledge of aid to Greece and Turkey, framed as a worldwide American commitment to defend 'free peoples' against communism."}, {"q": "What was the purpose of the Marshall Plan?", "a": "The 1947 aid program rebuilt western European economies to blunt communism's appeal; the Soviets refused to join, deepening Europe's division."}, {"q": "What was the Berlin blockade and airlift?", "a": "The 1948-1949 Soviet closure of land routes to West Berlin, answered by eleven months of Allied supply flights that forced Stalin to back down."}, {"q": "Why was NATO historically significant for the U.S.?", "a": "The 1949 mutual-defense alliance was America's first permanent European military commitment, ending its old aversion to entangling alliances."}, {"q": "What did NSC-68 call for?", "a": "The secret 1950 paper cast the Cold War as freedom versus slavery and urged a permanent, massive American military buildup."}, {"q": "Why did 1949 alarm American policymakers?", "a": "The Soviets tested an atomic bomb and Mao's communists won China, spurring calls for a huge arms buildup in NSC-68."}, {"q": "How did the Korean War begin and end?", "a": "It began when communist North Korea invaded the South in 1950; after Chinese intervention it ended in a 1953 armistice near the 38th parallel, not a peace treaty."}, {"q": "Why did Truman fire General MacArthur in 1951?", "a": "For insubordination, after MacArthur publicly challenged Truman's limited-war strategy in Korea and pushed to widen the conflict with China."}, {"q": "What was Truman's Fair Deal, and how did it fare?", "a": "His program of national health insurance, higher minimum wages, and expanded Social Security, most of which a conservative Congress blocked."}, {"q": "What did the Taft-Hartley Act do?", "a": "The 1947 law, passed over Truman's veto, banned the closed shop, allowed state right-to-work laws, and curbed union power."}, {"q": "How did Taft-Hartley affect organized labor long-term?", "a": "By weakening union strength and enabling right-to-work laws, it contributed to labor's long-term decline."}, {"q": "What civil rights steps did Truman take?", "a": "His commission issued To Secure These Rights, and he desegregated the armed forces in 1948, an unprecedented federal embrace of civil rights."}, {"q": "Who were the Dixiecrats?", "a": "Southern Democrats who bolted the 1948 convention over its civil rights plank and ran Strom Thurmond on a segregationist States' Rights ticket."}, {"q": "Why was Truman's 1948 win surprising?", "a": "Despite the Dixiecrat and Progressive splits and confident predictions of a Dewey victory, Truman stunned pollsters by winning."}, {"q": "What was McCarthyism?", "a": "The post-1950 wave of reckless anticommunist accusation led by Senator Joseph McCarthy, whose unproven charges of subversion destroyed careers."}, {"q": "Who were the Hollywood Ten?", "a": "Screenwriters and directors jailed in 1947 for refusing to answer HUAC's questions about their politics; studios blacklisted them and hundreds more."}, {"q": "What was the significance of the Rosenberg case?", "a": "Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were convicted of atomic espionage conspiracy and executed in 1953, fueling the era's climate of fear."}, {"q": "How did the second Red Scare affect civil liberties?", "a": "Loyalty oaths, the McCarran acts, and blacklists cost thousands their jobs and narrowed the boundaries of acceptable dissent for a generation."}, {"q": "How did the concept of 'totalitarianism' shape Cold War thinking?", "a": "By lumping the USSR with Nazi Germany, it framed American freedom as the opposite of totalitarian tyranny."}, {"q": "How did the Cold War redefine American freedom?", "a": "Official culture equated Americanism with anticommunism and 'free enterprise,' even accepting undemocratic allies as the 'Free World.'"}, {"q": "What was the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?", "a": "The 1948 UN document, drafted under Eleanor Roosevelt, proclaiming political liberties and social-economic entitlements as rights for all people."}, {"q": "What was HUAC and what did it do in Hollywood?", "a": "The House Un-American Activities Committee investigated alleged communist influence, most famously in its 1947 Hollywood hearings."}, {"q": "What ended McCarthy's power?", "a": "The televised 1954 Army-McCarthy hearings exposed his bullying, and the Senate voted to condemn him."}, {"q": "How did anticommunism affect the civil rights movement?", "a": "It purged militant unionists and pushed civil rights groups to distance themselves from the left to avoid subversion charges."}, {"q": "Why did civil rights advocates say segregation hurt the U.S. abroad?", "a": "They argued Jim Crow damaged America's image in the Cold War contest for hearts and minds against Soviet propaganda."}, {"q": "What did Jackie Robinson accomplish in 1947?", "a": "He integrated major league baseball, a landmark breakthrough against segregation during the early Cold War years."}], "24": [{"q": "What made the postwar era a 'golden age' of capitalism?", "a": "Between 1946 and 1960 the GNP more than doubled and real wages soared, letting most Americans live far better than their parents."}, {"q": "What was Levittown?", "a": "Mass-produced suburban developments pioneered by William Levitt, offering affordable prefab homes to whites while excluding Black buyers for years."}, {"q": "What fueled the postwar suburban boom?", "a": "The baby boom, cheap FHA and VA mortgages, and the automobile knit together by the new interstate highway system."}, {"q": "What was the baby boom?", "a": "The surge in U.S. births from the end of World War II into the mid-1960s, driving suburban growth and a huge youth generation."}, {"q": "How was the suburban dream racially closed?", "a": "Federal mortgage policy, private discrimination, and urban renewal confined Black Americans and Latinos to declining city neighborhoods."}, {"q": "What did urban renewal do to city neighborhoods?", "a": "Government programs bulldozed poor neighborhoods for highways and housing, displacing non-white residents who were shut out of the suburbs."}, {"q": "How was the interstate highway system justified?", "a": "Begun in 1956, the 41,000-mile network was partly justified by Cold War defense needs and transformed travel, commerce, and settlement."}, {"q": "What was the kitchen debate?", "a": "The 1959 Moscow exchange where Nixon told Khrushchev that consumer abundance and suburban domesticity proved capitalist freedom."}, {"q": "What was Eisenhower's Modern Republicanism?", "a": "An approach that accepted and even expanded core New Deal programs while courting business, legitimizing the New Deal state."}, {"q": "What was the labor-management 'social contract' of the 1950s?", "a": "An arrangement in which unions traded militancy for rising wages and benefits, securing prosperity for organized workers."}, {"q": "What was the doctrine of massive retaliation?", "a": "Dulles's threat of all-out nuclear response to Soviet aggression, which deterred war but bred fear of mutual assured destruction."}, {"q": "What CIA interventions extended containment into the Third World?", "a": "CIA-backed coups toppled elected governments in Iran (1953) and Guatemala (1954), and the U.S. propped up Diem in South Vietnam."}, {"q": "What did the Geneva Accords do to Vietnam?", "a": "The 1954 agreement temporarily divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel with unification elections planned for 1956—elections Diem refused to hold."}, {"q": "What was Sputnik and why did it matter?", "a": "The first artificial satellite, launched by the USSR in 1957; the shock spurred the space race and federal science funding via the NDEA."}, {"q": "What did Brown v. Board of Education decide?", "a": "The unanimous 1954 Warren Court ruling that segregated public schools are inherently unequal, overturning Plessy's 'separate but equal' in education."}, {"q": "Who argued Brown before the Supreme Court?", "a": "NAACP attorney Thurgood Marshall, who persuaded the Warren Court that segregated schools violated the Fourteenth Amendment."}, {"q": "What sparked the Montgomery bus boycott?", "a": "Rosa Parks's December 1955 arrest for refusing to give up her bus seat launched the 381-day Black boycott of Montgomery's buses."}, {"q": "How did the Montgomery bus boycott end and what was its impact?", "a": "The Supreme Court struck down bus segregation, and the victory made Martin Luther King Jr. a national leader."}, {"q": "What philosophy guided Martin Luther King Jr.'s leadership?", "a": "Drawing on Christian ethics and Gandhian nonviolence, he championed church-based nonviolent protest against segregation."}, {"q": "What was the SCLC?", "a": "The Southern Christian Leadership Conference, founded in 1957 under King to coordinate nonviolent campaigns against segregation across the South."}, {"q": "What was the Southern Manifesto?", "a": "A 1956 declaration signed by nearly all southern members of Congress denouncing Brown and endorsing resistance to integration."}, {"q": "What happened in the Little Rock crisis?", "a": "In 1957 Eisenhower reluctantly sent federal troops to escort nine Black students into Central High after Arkansas's governor and a mob blocked them."}, {"q": "Why did Little Rock show court victories weren't enough?", "a": "White massive resistance stalled Brown's enforcement, proving that ending Jim Crow required active federal power, not just rulings."}, {"q": "Who were the Beats?", "a": "A circle of 1950s writers like Allen Ginsberg who rejected suburban materialism, conformity, and Cold War militarism for spontaneity and personal liberation."}, {"q": "How did critics challenge 1950s conformity?", "a": "Writers like David Riesman, the Beats, and rebellious rock-and-roll youth questioned mass society and consensus culture."}, {"q": "What was Mendez v. Westminster and who won it?", "a": "A LULAC-backed case that struck down segregation of Mexican-American schoolchildren in California, foreshadowing Brown."}, {"q": "What did Eisenhower warn against in his Farewell Address?", "a": "He cautioned that the 'military-industrial complex'—an alliance of the military and a permanent arms industry—could endanger American democracy."}, {"q": "What wrecked the planned 1960 U.S.-Soviet summit?", "a": "The U-2 incident, in which the Soviets shot down an American spy plane, collapsed Eisenhower's cautious diplomacy."}, {"q": "How did the 1960 election turn out?", "a": "John F. Kennedy narrowly defeated Nixon after the first televised presidential debates, in a razor-thin victory."}, {"q": "What was the National Defense Education Act?", "a": "A 1958 law, spurred by Sputnik, providing the first major federal funding for higher education, especially science and math."}], "25": [{"q": "What event in February 1960 launched the decade's sit-in movement?", "a": "Four Black college students refused to leave a whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina, sparking a wave of sit-ins across the South."}, {"q": "Who guided the founding of SNCC in 1960, and what was its focus?", "a": "Ella Baker encouraged the creation of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, which empowered youth-led, grassroots protest by ordinary Black southerners."}, {"q": "What were the Freedom Rides and who organized them?", "a": "CORE's 1961 integrated bus trips through the Deep South tested court rulings against segregated interstate travel, enduring mob violence to win federal desegregation orders."}, {"q": "Why did the 1963 Birmingham campaign help push Kennedy toward a civil rights bill?", "a": "Televised images of police dogs and fire hoses used on peaceful protesters shocked the nation and world, pressuring Kennedy to endorse civil rights legislation."}, {"q": "What happened at the August 1963 March on Washington?", "a": "About 250,000 people gathered for 'Jobs and Freedom,' where King delivered his 'I Have a Dream' speech, capping the nonviolent phase of the movement."}, {"q": "Why is the Cuban missile crisis considered the Cold War's most dangerous moment?", "a": "The October 1962 standoff over Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba brought the superpowers closer to nuclear war than any other confrontation before ending peacefully."}, {"q": "How did Kennedy respond to the discovery of Soviet missiles in Cuba?", "a": "He imposed a naval blockade of Cuba and used secret diplomacy to secure Soviet withdrawal of the missiles."}, {"q": "What did the Civil Rights Act of 1964 accomplish?", "a": "It banned discrimination based on race and sex in employment and in public accommodations such as hotels, restaurants, and theaters."}, {"q": "Why was the Voting Rights Act of 1965 passed, and what did it do?", "a": "Passed after the violent Selma marches, it let federal officials register voters, breaking the South's systematic disenfranchisement of Black citizens."}, {"q": "Compare the Civil Rights Act of 1964 with the Voting Rights Act of 1965.", "a": "The 1964 Act targeted discrimination in jobs and public accommodations; the 1965 Act specifically secured Black access to the ballot through federal enforcement."}, {"q": "What was Johnson's Great Society?", "a": "His 1965–1967 program of federal activism that created Medicare, Medicaid, education funding, and new agencies—the biggest reform push since the New Deal."}, {"q": "What was the War on Poverty and how effective was it?", "a": "Johnson's 1964 anti-poverty crusade (Head Start, VISTA, food stamps); combined with growth, it cut the poverty rate from 22 to 13 percent."}, {"q": "Why was the Hart-Celler Act of 1965 significant for later U.S. demographics?", "a": "By ending national-origins quotas, it unexpectedly shifted future immigration toward Asia and Latin America, transforming the population."}, {"q": "Who popularized 'Black Power' and what did it mean?", "a": "Stokely Carmichael in 1966; it demanded Black self-determination, pride, and community control, embodied by the Black Panther Party."}, {"q": "Why did the Black freedom struggle fracture after 1965?", "a": "Urban uprisings like Watts, Malcolm X's separatism, and Black Power showed frustration that legal victories left economic inequality untouched."}, {"q": "What was the Port Huron Statement?", "a": "The 1962 manifesto of Students for a Democratic Society defining the New Left's vision of 'participatory democracy' and criticizing bureaucratic institutions."}, {"q": "How did the Gulf of Tonkin resolution expand Johnson's power?", "a": "Passed in 1964 after reported attacks on U.S. ships, it gave Johnson essentially unlimited authority to wage war in Vietnam."}, {"q": "Why did the Vietnam War shatter the liberal Cold War consensus?", "a": "Escalation to over half a million troops fueled a mass antiwar movement and undermined public faith in the government's honesty and judgment."}, {"q": "What role did SDS and the New Left play in the 1960s?", "a": "They led the student antiwar movement and demanded 'participatory democracy,' challenging bureaucratic institutions and mainstream politics."}, {"q": "How did Betty Friedan's The Feminine Mystique influence the era?", "a": "Her 1963 book exposed the frustration of educated housewives, helping ignite second-wave feminism and leading to NOW's founding in 1966."}, {"q": "What was the Stonewall uprising and why did it matter?", "a": "The 1969 riots after a police raid on a Greenwich Village gay bar launched the modern gay liberation movement."}, {"q": "Why was the Tet offensive a turning point even though it was repelled?", "a": "The surprise 1968 attacks across South Vietnam destroyed public confidence that the U.S. was winning the war, eroding support for it."}, {"q": "How did the civil rights movement inspire other movements?", "a": "Its model sparked a rights revolution: second-wave feminism, gay liberation, Chavez's farm workers, Native American activism, and environmentalism."}, {"q": "What rights did the Warren Court expand during the 1960s?", "a": "It broadened protections including Miranda warnings, privacy, one person one vote, criminal procedure, and interracial marriage."}, {"q": "What made 1968 a climactic and traumatic year?", "a": "The Tet offensive, the assassinations of King and Robert Kennedy, and chaos at the Chicago convention capped the decade's upheaval."}, {"q": "Why did Richard Nixon win the 1968 election?", "a": "His 'law and order' message appealed to voters weary of protest and disorder, signaling liberalism's retreat and a conservative resurgence."}, {"q": "What signs of rising conservatism appeared even amid 1960s liberalism?", "a": "Barry Goldwater's 1964 campaign and groups like Young Americans for Freedom foreshadowed the coming conservative movement."}, {"q": "What was Freedom Summer?", "a": "The 1964 voter-registration drive in Mississippi, in which activists—some of whom were murdered—organized Black voters despite violent resistance."}, {"q": "What Cold War initiatives and setbacks marked Kennedy's presidency?", "a": "He launched the Peace Corps and signed a test-ban treaty but stumbled at the Bay of Pigs and faced the Cuban missile crisis."}, {"q": "How did Roe v. Wade (1973) build on the Warren Court's legacy?", "a": "It established a constitutional right to abortion, extending the privacy rulings the Warren Court had developed in the 1960s."}], "26": [{"q": "How did Nixon govern domestically despite his conservative image?", "a": "He governed pragmatically, expanding the Great Society by creating the EPA and OSHA, broadening food stamps, and indexing Social Security."}, {"q": "What was détente and who shaped it?", "a": "Nixon and Kissinger's policy of easing tensions with the USSR and China, replacing rigid Cold War hostility with negotiation, trade, and arms control."}, {"q": "Why was Nixon's 1972 visit to China significant?", "a": "It opened relations with communist China after decades of hostility, a landmark of détente that reshaped Cold War diplomacy."}, {"q": "What did the SALT agreements accomplish?", "a": "The 1972 Strategic Arms Limitation Treaty capped nuclear missile arsenals and, with the ABM Treaty, barred missile-defense systems."}, {"q": "What were the Pentagon Papers and who leaked them?", "a": "A secret Defense Department history of the Vietnam War leaked by Daniel Ellsberg in 1971, revealing that presidents had misled the public."}, {"q": "Why did the 1970 Cambodia invasion lead to Kent State?", "a": "Nixon's expansion of the war into Cambodia sparked campus protests; National Guardsmen then killed four students at Kent State."}, {"q": "What was the War Powers Act of 1973?", "a": "A law requiring congressional approval for sending U.S. troops abroad, the strongest legislative check on presidential war-making in U.S. history."}, {"q": "What was the My Lai massacre?", "a": "The 1968 killing of hundreds of Vietnamese civilians by American troops; its 1969 exposure deepened public disillusionment with the war."}, {"q": "What was Watergate and how did it end Nixon's presidency?", "a": "A 1972 break-in at Democratic headquarters by Nixon operatives; the cover-up, revealed by tapes and hearings, forced his resignation in 1974."}, {"q": "Why did Vietnam and Watergate together strengthen conservatism?", "a": "They shattered Americans' trust in the federal government, bolstering conservative arguments that Washington's power should be limited."}, {"q": "What caused the 1973 oil embargo and its effects?", "a": "Arab OPEC states cut off exports to retaliate for Western support of Israel, causing gas shortages, soaring prices, and global recession."}, {"q": "Define stagflation and why it discredited liberal economics.", "a": "The 1970s mix of slow growth, high unemployment, and high inflation defied traditional liberal remedies, undermining Keynesian management."}, {"q": "What was deindustrialization and its consequences?", "a": "The collapse of manufacturing in older regions as firms automated and moved to low-wage areas, cutting real wages and weakening unions."}, {"q": "What is the Sunbelt and why did it matter politically?", "a": "The southern and western states that gained population, jobs, and clout after WWII, becoming a stronghold of modern conservatism."}, {"q": "What did Regents v. Bakke (1978) decide about affirmative action?", "a": "It struck down fixed racial quotas in admissions but allowed race as one factor among many, remaining a touchstone for affirmative action law."}, {"q": "What were the Camp David Accords?", "a": "The 1978 Egypt-Israel peace framework brokered by President Carter, a landmark of his human-rights-centered diplomacy."}, {"q": "What was the Moral Majority and who founded it?", "a": "Jerry Falwell's 1979 Religious Right organization that mobilized evangelical Christians behind 'pro-family' conservative candidates and causes."}, {"q": "Why was Proposition 13 significant?", "a": "California's 1978 initiative froze property taxes, a signal victory of the national tax revolt that fed conservative politics."}, {"q": "How did the Iranian hostage crisis and other 1979 events hurt Carter?", "a": "The hostage seizure, a second oil shock, Three Mile Island, and the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan made Carter appear helpless."}, {"q": "What groups made up the new conservative coalition?", "a": "Neoconservative intellectuals, the evangelical Religious Right, anti-tax rebels, Sunbelt suburbanites, and opponents of the ERA and abortion."}, {"q": "Why did the conservative coalition oppose the ERA?", "a": "'Family values' activists like Phyllis Schlafly argued it threatened traditional gender roles, and their mobilization defeated ratification."}, {"q": "What was Reaganomics?", "a": "Reagan's supply-side program of deep tax cuts, deregulation, and tight money; it tamed inflation and spurred growth but tripled the national debt."}, {"q": "How did Reaganomics affect inequality and deficits?", "a": "It revived growth and curbed inflation, but income inequality widened sharply and the national debt tripled during Reagan's presidency."}, {"q": "Why did Reagan fire the striking air traffic controllers in 1981?", "a": "He dismissed 13,000 PATCO strikers to enforce his anti-union stance, a signal of declining labor power under conservative policy."}, {"q": "What was the Iran-Contra affair?", "a": "A Reagan-era scandal in which officials secretly sold arms to Iran and illegally funneled proceeds to Nicaraguan Contras despite a congressional ban."}, {"q": "How did Reagan surprise critics on Cold War diplomacy?", "a": "After a massive military buildup, he negotiated historic arms reductions with Mikhail Gorbachev, easing superpower tensions."}, {"q": "What did the 1987 agreement between Reagan and Gorbachev do?", "a": "The two leaders agreed to eliminate intermediate-range nuclear missiles, a major arms-reduction breakthrough."}, {"q": "Compare Carter's and Reagan's foreign policies.", "a": "Carter centered human rights and diplomacy (Camp David); Reagan revived Cold War confrontation and covert action before later arms-reduction deals."}, {"q": "How had conservatism reshaped politics by 1988?", "a": "It had captured the language of freedom—limited government, low taxes, traditional values—setting the terms of national politics for both parties."}, {"q": "What did Gerald Ford's brief presidency accomplish, and how did his pardon affect him?", "a": "Ford produced little beyond the Helsinki Accords; his pardon of Nixon damaged his standing and contributed to his 1976 defeat."}], "27": [{"q": "What happened to the Soviet bloc in 1989–1991?", "a": "Communist regimes across eastern Europe peacefully collapsed, the Berlin Wall fell, and by the end of 1991 the USSR dissolved into fifteen nations."}, {"q": "What did George H. W. Bush mean by a 'new world order'?", "a": "His vision of the post–Cold War era organized around democracy and global free trade under American leadership."}, {"q": "What was the Gulf War of 1991 and its outcome?", "a": "A U.S.-led coalition drove Iraq out of Kuwait; the quick victory boosted Bush's approval but left Saddam Hussein in power."}, {"q": "Why did the Gulf War spark debate among policymakers?", "a": "It showcased American power but raised the question of whether the U.S. should act as a global policeman in the post–Cold War order."}, {"q": "Why did Bush lose the 1992 election despite Gulf War popularity?", "a": "A recession undercut his soaring approval, and Clinton won a three-way race in which Ross Perot took nearly a fifth of the vote."}, {"q": "What was NAFTA and who opposed it?", "a": "The 1993 free-trade agreement eliminating tariffs among the U.S., Canada, and Mexico; unions and environmentalists warned it would export jobs."}, {"q": "How did Clinton respond after his health-care plan collapsed and the GOP won Congress?", "a": "He co-opted conservative themes, declaring 'the era of big government is over' and signing the 1996 abolition of AFDC welfare."}, {"q": "What was the Contract with America?", "a": "Newt Gingrich's 1994 GOP platform promising smaller government, tax cuts, and welfare overhaul; it helped Republicans capture Congress."}, {"q": "What drove the 1990s economic boom, and what were its downsides?", "a": "Computers, the Internet, and globalization produced low unemployment and surpluses, but inequality widened and the dot-com bubble later burst."}, {"q": "Why was the 1999 repeal of Glass-Steagall significant?", "a": "It removed Depression-era banking restrictions, unleashing risky financial practices that helped set the stage for later crises."}, {"q": "What was the Battle of Seattle?", "a": "The December 1999 protests by some 30,000 labor, environmental, and human-rights activists against the WTO, spotlighting globalization's costs."}, {"q": "How did the U.S. respond differently to Rwanda and the Balkans?", "a": "Clinton stood by during the 1994 Rwandan genocide but joined NATO's 1999 intervention against ethnic cleansing in Kosovo."}, {"q": "Define ethnic cleansing and where the term gained global use.", "a": "The violent expulsion of an ethnic group from a territory; the term spread during the wars following Yugoslavia's breakup."}, {"q": "How did post-1965 immigration reshape American society?", "a": "Tens of millions of newcomers, mostly from Latin America and Asia, made Latinos the largest minority and diversified the nation."}, {"q": "What were the Culture Wars of the 1990s?", "a": "Battles over abortion, gay rights, school curricula, and family values that pitted religious conservatives against secular, multicultural America."}, {"q": "Define multiculturalism and the debate around it.", "a": "A 1990s emphasis on ethnic group identity and diverse institutions—celebrated by some, blamed by others for national fragmentation."}, {"q": "What did the Americans with Disabilities Act (1990) do?", "a": "It extended the rights revolution by banning disability discrimination in employment and requiring accessible public buildings."}, {"q": "What sparked the 1992 Los Angeles (Rodney King) riots?", "a": "The acquittal of police officers who were videotaped beating a Black motorist triggered the deadliest U.S. uprising since the draft riots."}, {"q": "What was the Oklahoma City bombing?", "a": "Antigovernment extremist Timothy McVeigh destroyed a federal building in 1995, killing 168 and exposing the danger of far-right militias."}, {"q": "Why was Clinton impeached, and what was the result?", "a": "The House charged him in 1998 with perjury and obstruction over the Lewinsky affair; the Senate acquitted him in 1999 and his popularity held."}, {"q": "What did Bush v. Gore decide?", "a": "The 5-4 Supreme Court ruling of December 2000 halted Florida's recount, giving Bush the presidency though Gore won the popular vote."}, {"q": "Why was the 2000 election a sign of a strained democracy?", "a": "The disputed outcome, settled by the Court, revealed a nation split almost evenly and flaws in the electoral process."}, {"q": "What was Al Qaeda and what grievances motivated it?", "a": "Osama bin Laden's terrorist network, angered by U.S. bases in Saudi Arabia and support for Israel, which carried out the 9/11 attacks."}, {"q": "What happened on September 11, 2001?", "a": "Al Qaeda hijackers attacked the World Trade Center and Pentagon, killing about 3,000 people and shattering America's post–Cold War security."}, {"q": "How did Clinton consolidate the conservative turn in politics?", "a": "He embraced free trade, balanced budgets, and welfare abolition while defending social liberalism, ratifying the rightward shift."}, {"q": "What ended the Cold War, and what was America's resulting global role?", "a": "The peaceful collapse of communism (1989–1991) left the U.S. the sole superpower, opening most of the world to market capitalism."}, {"q": "What happened at Tiananmen Square in 1989?", "a": "Chinese troops crushed pro-democracy protests, a stark contrast to the peaceful collapse of communism in eastern Europe that same year."}, {"q": "How did the 1996 welfare reform change public assistance?", "a": "Clinton signed a law abolishing AFDC, replacing guaranteed aid with work requirements and time limits, ending a New Deal-era entitlement."}, {"q": "What evidence showed racial inequality persisted in the 1990s despite legal equality?", "a": "Soaring mass incarceration, continued housing segregation, and the 1992 L.A. riots revealed enduring racial injustice."}, {"q": "Compare Bush's and Clinton's paths to the presidency in 1992.", "a": "Bush, weakened by recession, lost as an incumbent; Clinton won by blending social liberalism with promises to shrink government and reform welfare."}], "28": [{"q": "How did George W. Bush open his presidency before 9/11?", "a": "He pushed huge tax cuts favoring the wealthy and rejected the Kyoto climate treaty."}, {"q": "What was the Bush Doctrine?", "a": "Bush's post-9/11 foreign policy declaring a worldwide war on terrorism: nations were either with the United States or with the terrorists."}, {"q": "Why did the U.S. invade Afghanistan in 2001?", "a": "To destroy Al Qaeda and oust the Taliban regime that sheltered Osama bin Laden; it became the longest war in American history."}, {"q": "What powers did the USA Patriot Act grant?", "a": "The 2001 law gave law enforcement sweeping authority to wiretap, monitor communications, and gather personal records to prevent terrorism."}, {"q": "What was controversial about Guantánamo Bay?", "a": "Terrorism suspects were detained indefinitely without normal legal protections from 2002; the Supreme Court later affirmed their right to challenge imprisonment."}, {"q": "Why did the 2003 Iraq War isolate the U.S. internationally?", "a": "It was launched on false claims about weapons of mass destruction and without UN approval, then descended into insurgency and sectarian violence."}, {"q": "How did the Iraq War draw comparisons to Vietnam?", "a": "Baghdad fell quickly, but a prolonged insurgency and sectarian bloodshed turned it into a costly, unpopular occupation that isolated America."}, {"q": "How did Hurricane Katrina damage Bush's reputation?", "a": "The 2005 storm flooded New Orleans, and the government's inept response, which left poor Black residents stranded, destroyed his image of competence."}, {"q": "What did Lawrence v. Texas signal about the 1960s social revolution?", "a": "By striking down sodomy laws, the Court indicated that the era's expansion of personal rights and social change was permanent."}, {"q": "Why did the security-versus-liberty tension revive after 9/11?", "a": "The Patriot Act, Guantánamo detentions, torture, and mass surveillance tested constitutional limits, which the Supreme Court partially reasserted."}, {"q": "What caused the 2008 financial collapse?", "a": "Years of low interest rates, subprime lending, and unregulated Wall Street speculation burst when the housing bubble popped and Lehman Brothers failed."}, {"q": "What was the 2008 bank bailout?", "a": "A $700 billion federal commitment to rescue banks and firms deemed 'too big to fail' after Lehman Brothers' collapse."}, {"q": "Why is the Great Recession historically significant?", "a": "Beginning in December 2007, it became the worst economic downturn since the Great Depression and discredited market fundamentalism."}, {"q": "Why was Barack Obama's 2008 election a milestone?", "a": "He became the first African-American president, elected amid the financial crisis with a message of hope and change."}, {"q": "What did the Affordable Care Act (2010) do?", "a": "Obama's health law required individuals to carry insurance, subsidized coverage for those of modest means, and barred rejecting applicants, insuring millions."}, {"q": "Why was passing the ACA politically difficult?", "a": "It faced unanimous Republican opposition and fueled the Tea Party backlash, yet still extended coverage to millions."}, {"q": "What was the Tea Party movement?", "a": "A grassroots conservative movement arising in 2009 against Obama's spending and health-care programs that pushed the GOP rightward."}, {"q": "How did the 2010 elections produce gridlock?", "a": "The Tea Party backlash helped Republicans capture the House, generating lasting partisan gridlock with the Obama administration."}, {"q": "What did Occupy Wall Street protest?", "a": "The 2011 movement, launched in New York's financial district, denounced the wealth and power of 'the 1 percent' and spotlighted inequality."}, {"q": "What is Black Lives Matter?", "a": "A movement arising from the deaths of unarmed Black Americans at police hands, using social media to demand accountability and assert Black humanity."}, {"q": "What did Edward Snowden reveal in 2013?", "a": "The former NSA contractor leaked documents exposing massive government surveillance of Americans' phone and Internet communications."}, {"q": "What did Obergefell v. Hodges (2015) establish?", "a": "The Supreme Court decision, written by Justice Kennedy, created a constitutional right to same-sex marriage nationwide."}, {"q": "Who was Sonia Sotomayor?", "a": "Obama's 2009 Supreme Court appointee, the first Hispanic justice and third woman to serve on the Court."}, {"q": "What is ISIS?", "a": "The brutal Islamist insurgency that seized territory in Iraq, Syria, and Libya beginning in 2014, murdering minorities and sponsoring terror abroad."}, {"q": "How did Obama wind down the war on terror's early conflicts?", "a": "He ended the Iraq War in 2011 and ordered the raid that killed Osama bin Laden that same year."}, {"q": "Why did Obama's surveillance record disappoint some supporters?", "a": "Despite ending the Iraq War, he continued mass surveillance policies that Edward Snowden exposed in 2013."}, {"q": "How did the 2008 crisis's recovery affect inequality?", "a": "The recovery flowed overwhelmingly to the wealthiest Americans, making rising inequality a defining national issue."}, {"q": "What did the Supreme Court do to the Voting Rights Act in 2013?", "a": "It gutted a key section requiring federal preclearance of voting changes, weakening protections against discriminatory voting laws."}, {"q": "How significant were Obama's domestic achievements historically?", "a": "The stimulus, Affordable Care Act, and financial reform were the most significant domestic legislation since Johnson's Great Society."}, {"q": "What did the 2006 immigration protests reveal about national debate?", "a": "Battles over undocumented immigration brought millions of protesters into the streets, keeping border and deportation policy central to politics."}]};
